// AUTO-GENERATED by scripts/build-bible-v205.ts — do not edit by hand.
// Words: Jonathan's canonical commentary txt. Structure: his Notion pages.
import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "1 John",
  display: "First John",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have a few assumptions about this book that I think we should address before getting too deep into it. First is the assumption of the writer. We are going to see that whoever the writer is of this book was a man who spent quite a bit of time with Jesus. It is also very well associated with the Gospel of John because of this theme of love, light, and life that the writer of John’s Gospel was very focused on. We will also see that it aligns with similar writing as the Book of Revelation. Combining these three elements together, we can have an assumed author."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The Revelation of Jesus Christ, which God gave unto him, to shew unto his servants things which must shortly come to pass; and he sent and signified it by his angel unto his servant John: Who bare record of the word of God, and of the testimony of Jesus Christ, and of all things that he saw.","ref":"Revelation 1:1-2"},{"kind":"para","text":"In Revelation, John introduces himself for the first time as the writer. These other letters are just stated as an “Elder.” I assume John is quite old at this point of writing. He’s walked with Jesus, learned from Jesus, and has grown in His faith with Jesus. When we come to these three letters, we should recognize that John is not making himself out to be some sort of super-authority. Though he likely could have, seeing as he knows quite a lot about the faith of Christ. He is a forefather of the church after all. But the purpose of these letters will keep John humble, which brings us to our second assumption: What was this letter for?"},{"kind":"para","text":"This letter was not just to write greetings to a church. I have heard a ministry friend of mine say that he stays away from these books of the Bible because it’s too confusing to him. I think the books you are afraid to study the most are the books that describe what you are missing the most in your faith. For him, it was perhaps a love for the saints. We are going to see quite a bit of that talked about through these books. That is why John doesn’t call himself anything more than an “Elder.” He is one of the saints; he is just old. He is not the ultimate authority. He is the man who has simply learned a thing or two in his faith walk."},{"kind":"para","text":"Another assumption we should clarify before diving into this book is the religious application. There are far too many people who open this book and completely twist the intentions of John here. The church (or churches) that John is writing to here are not simply learning about God’s love. We are going to see that as a major theme, but the problem was not with being “love.” The problem was with giving love. From the sounds of it, this church was not very loving to those in the faith. I think that is a general theme with most legalist churches today. They love God, but they don’t show that same love for the saints."},{"kind":"para","text":"Perhaps the opposite can be true as well. I know of some churches whose only theme is love. God is nothing but love. There is no judgment. There is no change. There is no need to talk about sin or troubles. If you do not love, you are not godly. That is the wrong way to think about the faith as well. John will be very specific in the way he approaches these topics of love, and we should really dig into these words so we are not tricked into thinking that faith is only run by love. Faith is run by grace and love, to be sure, but it’s not the only factor that we should consider as important."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":4,"title":"Community & Joy","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This almost starts the same way as the gospel of John:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. The same was in the beginning with God. All things were made by him; and without him was not any thing made that was made. In him was life; and the life was the light of men.","ref":"John 1:1-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"We have discussions about God being the word and Him being life since the beginning. But what is the beginning of? The beginning of time, the beginning of faith, or the beginning of our ends? There are only a handful of people who could take this literally during this time. John (the Revelator) was one of them, and so it is sufficient to assume that this was likely him writing."},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, this first verse does nothing but point to Jesus Christ as being “the Word of life.” When we mix that in with John saying in the gospel account that the Word became flesh, we are assured that this fits perfectly with the character of Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us, (and we beheld his glory, the glory as of the only begotten of the Father,) full of grace and truth.","ref":"John 1:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"But even if you don’t want to take into account that John is speaking about Jesus here (capital “W” by the way), we can be assured that God’s Word is living and breathing. We have heard it, we have seen it, we have looked upon it, and we have handled it with our own hands."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This verse further indicates that the “Word” was Jesus Christ. It is only through God’s Word that we can have eternal life. It is only through His Word that anything, including Jesus, can be manifested. He makes it happen because He is God. He creates, and He destroys. He has all power and authority."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For with God nothing shall be impossible.","ref":"Luke 1:37"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, I am the LORD, the God of all flesh: is there any thing too hard for me?","ref":"Jeremiah 32:27"},{"kind":"para","text":"John was very good about pointing everything back to Christ and God’s final Words of manifestation. God has the power, the authority, and the glory. God is what John will point us to, and God is what we should be focusing on throughout these three letters, as well as in every chapter of scripture."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John is clarifying here that he is not going to talk about anything that he doesn’t actually know about or has seen with his own eyes. Christ’s ministry, salvation, and ascension were not just word of mouth to John. He actually saw these things with his own eyes and was a partaker of these things with his own hands and feet. He didn’t just write the gospel as someone who happened to see things; he wrote the gospel as someone involved with the things Jesus did. He was very close to Christ and even considered himself to be the one that Jesus loved. But we talked about that in the Gospel of John."},{"kind":"para","text":"An important note to grasp from this verse, though, is the word “fellowship.” “Fellowship” means “joint participation” or “community.” That is really the main theme of John’s letters here. He is going to talk about Love, Light, and God’s eternal being, but it’s all for the greater cause of community on Earth. We may not be in agreement with everything while we are here, but we should be in community with these three aspects of the Lord that John will speak about. If this book is too complicated for you to get your head around, you must go back to the Gospel of John and read through it to really grasp the character and person of Jesus. Modern Christianity paints different pictures of Jesus. John is going to paint a single picture that might go against a lot of what you hear or think about the Lord."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"An interesting term is used here. John says, “we” are writing to these churches. Who is “we”? Well, quite frankly, I don’t know. Personally, I believe it’s a church in which John is leading at the moment, who have had a good example of fellowship and joy. That is likely why he finds reason to write to this church. He is giving them some useful information that he is learning in this other church because he sees this church (that he’s writing to) falling into some sort of heartless worship of the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"Another very important theme that John will be writing about (according to himself) is the theme of joy. So, if by the end of these letters, we do not experience unity in community and joy in community, we will have to restart and reread this letter. We will have missed something."}]}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"title":"God Is Light","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There is an obvious theme of light throughout scripture. John seems to have grasped that after coming to know Jesus himself. Perhaps that is the only way to see light in scripture as well. I’ve known quite a few atheists who see scripture as dark and gloomy. They find some interesting information in it, but they don’t see it as factual, and they certainly don’t see it as useful. They don’t allow the scriptures to change their heart."},{"kind":"para","text":"Light is the starting place for a changed man. If we can’t see what needs to be changed, we are in darkness, and there is no way for us to change it without the light illuminating it. Light shines on sin. Christ is the light, and if we cannot agree with Him about sin, we will not have light that can prove to us our need for change. People can only read scripture in darkness because they don’t have Christ first."},{"kind":"para","text":"But once you have Christ, the light that shines out of scripture is unavoidable. Christ’s message is: God is light. If you don’t see the light of God in scripture, you will never read scriptures correctly. That is why so many interpretations come out of the Bible. Much of this world is reading their Bibles in the dark."}]}]},{"start":6,"end":10,"title":"Sins Forgiven","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We are going to encounter a few discrepancies that we will hopefully overcome while we read this. Many people read this and say, “This is talking about sin.” In a way, yes, it is talking about sin. But let’s remove sin from the picture for one moment (hang with me here). If everyone lived a life without sin in this world, we still would not have light. We might have false light, perhaps, but it’s not eternal light. Only God can provide eternal light. There’s something that glows in a person who is genuinely following the Lord. I find a lot of older Christians losing that glow. They don’t have joy. They don’t care about community. They just want to be right more than they want to be light. But the light only shines in what is truth. Everything else is a lie, and those things rest in darkness."},{"kind":"para","text":"We face a dark world today. We face a dark world because so many people claim they love the Lord, but they walk in darkness. They are lying about their love of God. Their lies are all they can see in the dark. So instead of illuminating the truth, they look for “new” truths and reject the light. Not only do they reject the truth, but John says they “do not the truth.” Not even their actions are true or genuine."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In order to see the light of God, we must agree with the Word of God. We cannot come to God’s Word and say, “Hmm. I don’t like this. I won’t follow this. I won’t study this. I won’t believe this. I won’t even try to utilize this.” When we do that, we are doing nothing but rejecting the light of God. We can only walk in the light if we have Christ, who is the light."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then spake Jesus again unto them, saying, I am the light of the world: he that followeth me shall not walk in darkness, but shall have the light of life.","ref":"John 8:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"I have to give you news if you are struggling with this verse; if you don’t feel like you are always in the light, that does not mean you are not a Christian. You will experience seasons of darkness. This is just a warning. However, you can still walk with the light. Be drawn to it. Don’t just reject the light because there is darkness all around you. John will clarify even further in the next verse. This is another verse that a lot of people misinterpret:"}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This and verse 9 go very well together. However, I want to take a moment to share some truths with you (the reader) to provide hope and assurance. Everyone is a sinner. Period. We have plenty of scripture to insinuate this:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God;","ref":"Romans 3:23"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.","ref":"Romans 5:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The Lord is not slack concerning his promise, as some men count slackness; but is longsuffering to us-ward, not willing that any should perish, but that all should come to repentance.","ref":"2 Peter 3:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For there is not a just man upon earth, that doeth good, and sinneth not.","ref":"Ecclesiastes 7:20"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For whosoever shall keep the whole law, and yet offend in one point, he is guilty of all.","ref":"James 2:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Ye are of your father the devil, and the lusts of your father ye will do. He was a murderer from the beginning, and abode not in the truth, because there is no truth in him. When he speaketh a lie, he speaketh of his own: for he is a liar, and the father of it.","ref":"John 8:44"},{"kind":"para","text":"We can read the entire Old Testament account to see the sin of man quite prominently."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore, as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned:","ref":"Romans 5:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"We see that sin is a very real thing in the lives of humanity. We can observe it in our own children as well, when they make grave errors like lying, disobeying, bullying, hurting, torturing, and being mean to other children. Not to boast, but I’ve done a pretty good job at training my daughter not to be a bully. Yet, there are times when I see her do things that are not right and kind to other people. That is sin. It rears its ugly head in even the strongest spiritual leaders. Your pastor is a sinner. Your priest is a sinner. Your godly friends are sinners. Your church is filled with sinners. That is just the truth."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, it is not our sin that defines us. We might struggle with sin. We might hurt ourselves with sin. But thankfully, we have a good God who loves us enough to forgive our sins and to set our actions and motives right. John will speak about that next:"}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"When we confess our sins to Him, God forgives us and cleanses us. That does not mean we will never sin again. It means when we do sin, we have a way to make it right. Just like if you do something to hurt someone else, that damage doesn’t have to remain permanent. You can go to that person and try to make it right. If they don’t want to hear your apology, well, you can move on from there, but at least you tried."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you come to God in real and necessary repentance, according to John, your sins will be forgiven. Not just forgiven, but cleansed. He will not just leave you hanging in your sin guilt. If you still feel guilty from your sin after God forgave you, you are bringing it upon yourself. You cannot place any guilt on the Lord when He has gladly forgiven you for what you have done."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, God has made it very clear that we are all sinners. God’s Word illuminates that to the Christian. We are not condemned by God’s Word; we are forgiven and cleansed. God’s Word, to the believer, lights up with forgiveness."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you only see God’s judgment and wrath through His Word, it might be because you are worried that it’s what you deserve. That is not what a child of God should be focused on, though. We will read about that in the next chapters."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10}],"verses":{"1":"That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled, of the Word of life;","2":"(For the life was manifested, and we have seen it, and bear witness, and shew unto you that eternal life, which was with the Father, and was manifested unto us;)","3":"That which we have seen and heard declare we unto you, that ye also may have fellowship with us: and truly our fellowship is with the Father, and with his Son Jesus Christ.","4":"And these things write we unto you, that your joy may be full.","5":"This then is the message which we have heard of him, and declare unto you, that God is light, and in him is no darkness at all.","6":"If we say that we have fellowship with him, and walk in darkness, we lie, and do not the truth:","7":"But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin.","8":"If we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us.","9":"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.","10":"If we say that we have not sinned, we make him a liar, and his word is not in us."}},
    "2": {"chapter":2,"intro":[],"previously":["In our last chapter, John opened this book with a somewhat short introduction to the church. He spoke about writing to them to help increase their faith and unity through remembering and living in the light and love of the Lord. We learned that the theme of this letter was to keep these members in the faith of Christ through their love towards each other and their love towards God. In this chapter, we will get a closer look at how this church was really organized and how they were treating each other in the “faith.” We will also see some similarities to people leaving the faith against Christ and how that is affected by how people treat each other in the church. Those things walk pretty close together. I want to do my best to keep the theme of John here by addressing the love of God, the light of God, and the light of God through these chapters."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":6,"title":"Little Children","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Notice the way in which John is addressing this church. He is calling them his “children.” Now, there are a few in the faith who have referred to some of the people they are in ministry with as their “child” in the faith."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Thou therefore, my son, be strong in the grace that is in Christ Jesus.","ref":"2 Timothy 2:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Unto Timothy, my own son in the faith: Grace, mercy, and peace, from God our Father and Jesus Christ our Lord.","ref":"1 Timothy 1:2"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The church that is at Babylon, elected together with you, saluteth you; and so doth Marcus my son.","ref":"1 Peter 5:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"John will clarify this deeper in the next chapter, but I think we must understand the tone of the book so we can understand John’s compassion for this church. He is calling them his “little” children. Not because this is a young church, not because they are simply younger than him, and not even because they are mostly children. Rather, he calls them “little” children because they are acting like children. If children are going to learn and grow, they need to be taught, and so he is approaching this church in a respectful fatherly way as if they are children in need of correction."},{"kind":"para","text":"He will not scold and destroy their conduct and methodology (though he probably has the authority from the Lord to do so). Rather, he is going to show them the example of Christ. This is how God deals with His own. We are the children of God (as John will say in the next chapter). In Genesis, we read about an account somewhat similar to this:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That the sons of God saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they took them wives of all which they chose","ref":"Genesis 6:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"We discussed how these were not angels having children with people, but rather people in the lineage of God’s chosen (the lineage of Seth) who were having children with women who were not in God’s lineage (the daughters of Caine). They were saved men, people who looked for the Lord and followed Him. However, they were doing something wrong by marrying these daughters of men because they were enticing them away from the Lord. That is a lesson we can discuss in a different setting."},{"kind":"para","text":"The point is, John wants to talk to this church as if he were their father figure, the same way the Lord will talk to His own children. He’s not saying that you simply won’t sin ever again if you belong to the Lord. Rather, you still have plenty of chances to sin. But he’s writing this to this church so that they don’t do it on purpose. They must know what sin is and must openly choose to repent of it in order to have an advocate with the Father. If they don’t find a need for repentance, they are likely not in the family of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Every child of God will feel conviction for sin and will desire to keep short accounts with the Lord because they love Him too much to continue to live against Him. If you know what He did for you, there is no way in this world you would ever want to purposely hurt Him."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"“Propitiation” means “appeasing.” Here’s a verse that will have the Calvinists mooing. They don’t like that term “world.” Everything is anti-world in the movement of a Calvinist. I understand why they don’t like the world. We are told several times to shun the world; in fact, we will talk about that very thing in this chapter later on."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, what should be understood is that Jesus died for the sinner—those that are in the world. I truly believe that Christ died for all of humanity. The only people that will be saved, however, are those that accept that gift of salvation. If they are not willing to turn to the Lord for salvation, the gift lies dormant to them. They have the ability; they just need to take the gift. We’ve spoken about that in several settings as well, and we will talk about that until the end of this book."},{"kind":"para","text":"The point is, Christ is a propitiation for the sins of the entire world, but only those that turn to Him will have their sins forgiven."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I think we are in great error to assume that John is talking about the Ten Commandments in the Old Testament. Those commandments are for the lost to come to Christ. When we are in Christ, it is not the Ten Commandments that hold our salvation. No, John says we live by the commands of Christ if we belong to Him. Christ’s commands are quite simple. We are to love God and love others. How we do that is labeled throughout all of the New Testament, starting with Jesus’ ministry. He gave us the best examples of how to do that. He served others, He loved others, He died for others. He served God, He loved God, He died for God. Paul tells us a few ways we can do it, but he demonstrates the difference best in 1 Thessalonians:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For ye know what commandments we gave you by the Lord Jesus.","ref":"1 Thessalonians 4:2"},{"kind":"para","text":"The commandments of Christ are far more rewarding than the Ten Commandments could ever be. If you want to live a legalistic life, follow the Ten Commandments. If you want to live a freed life, follow the Lord’s Commandments."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christ lived very opposite to the world. He loved the people that everyone else shunned. Honestly, that’s why a lot of people hated Him. He wasn’t doing what they were doing. He wasn’t associating Himself with their religious ideology."},{"kind":"para","text":"That is why the world will hate you too. They will hate you because you don’t act and live like they do. But if we truly know Him, we will live like Him. We will illuminate with the actions of Christ, not just hold in the Word of God."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John focused quite heavily on the words of Christ. He was very focused on who Jesus was. It was about His actions, not just about His proclamation. It’s one thing for me to say “I am the Son of God” and a far different thing for me to prove it. John picked up on Christ’s proof nearly instantly. He believed it right from the start."},{"kind":"para","text":"So when he says that someone should keep Christ’s words, he truly knows what that looks like and means. It’s not just about preaching a good message and believing a good thing. It’s about actually displaying Christ in the way you love others. It pours out, whether you want it to or not."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Christ says that His disciples should live IN Him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Abide in me, and I in you. As the branch cannot bear fruit of itself, except it abide in the vine; no more can ye, except ye abide in me. I am the vine, ye are the branches: He that abideth in me, and I in him, the same bringeth forth much fruit: for without me ye can do nothing.","ref":"John 15:4-5"},{"kind":"para","text":"We should live in the Lord if we want to be more like the Lord. If we are not displaying Christ in action and in word, we are not truly living in Christ. There are a lot of really great preachers who bark a lot behind the pulpit. When it comes down to it, however, they don’t bite. They don’t really care to live what they preach. They don’t really care to live like the Lord. They are “soldiers” in and out of the church. They are not doctors or healers. They want to shoot; they don’t want to save."},{"kind":"para","text":"The only reason I know this is because I was groomed to think this way too. I’ve seen it in my own life and in a lot of other church leaders’ lives. The real problem comes, however, when they start shooting their own people. That is where John is going with this. He doesn’t see unity in the church. He sees these people shooting each other when they should be loving each other and growing with each other. They are not walking like Christ. They might say godly things, but their hearts are not in the right place."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":8,"title":"A New Commandment","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I believe John is writing to a church that he certainly helped start. They were on fire for the Lord when he left there. Now he’s hearing accounts of a church with no light or love for itself. Perhaps they had a love for the lost, but they certainly didn’t have a love for each other. I’ve seen too many churches fall into this trap. It is not healthy. Arguably, if the church is not in love with the Lord and with each other, they don’t have the light of the Lord anymore either. Love and light go together. Without one or the other, you can’t have either. You won’t have life as well. But we will get into that later. This is something they should know very well though. It was taught to them in the beginning of their creation, but they seem to have lost it. So John has to remind them."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is another pretty obvious claim that the commandments that John is talking about are only from Christ and not of the Ten Commandments for the sinner. John can’t make new commandments from the law of God:"}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What's the new commandment?"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A new commandment I give unto you, That ye love one another; as I have loved you, that ye also love one another. By this shall all men know that ye are my disciples, if ye have love one to another.","ref":"John 13:34-35"},{"kind":"para","text":"Before you are saved, you have a rough idea of what you think love should be. After you are saved, however, you realize that the idea you had before was very far from what God intended love to be. It is a sacrifice. A literal giving of your own life. That is real love."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s not a ring. It’s not a marriage. It’s not an idea, or feeling. It’s not an image, or a physical attribute. It’s sacrifice. Furthermore, He proved ultimate love by dying for people who don’t even care about it. Again, I believe He died for the whole world. We have several testimonies of this in the New Testament, but perhaps we can just take Christ’s own word for it:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life.","ref":"John 3:16"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"This is a faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief.","ref":"1 Timothy 1:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God;","ref":"Romans 3:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"As we can see, the world means everyone, whether they take the gift or not. That does not mean His gift of salvation was wasted. It simply means He gave the open invite to all, and it is up to the individual to accept it or decline it. But no one can ever say that Jesus doesn’t love them. The truth is far too obvious to deny."},{"kind":"para","text":"This should not be a “new” commandment to anyone, but it is new to people if they now have the Lord. Loving others should look like sacrifice, otherwise it is not love."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":11,"title":"Hating Brothers","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Again, love and light walk together. You can’t have one without the other. I think there are quite a few people in my life who have claimed themselves to Christ who absolutely hate me. We are of the same family (in Christ), and they don’t like me at all. I can’t say I feel that way about anyone else. It hurts to be hated, especially if they are people who claim to be Christians. Even worse is if they are pastors. They claim to walk in the light, but they can’t truly do that if they hate their brother."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You love a brother by continually helping, encouraging, and being faithful to them. If a pastor, or “brother in Christ,” has given up on you, they did not care about you. They didn’t actually love you. I’m just being honest. Furthermore, a brother who truly loves you will not make you stumble in your faith. Those people that I mentioned hate me have tried to keep me from doing ministry ever again. They have tried to make me stumble a lot. I have stumbled. I’ve even fallen."},{"kind":"para","text":"I remember when I had left Texas and came back to California, I was out on the streets in the middle of the night, sitting on a curb in a random neighborhood just crying to God. I told Him, “Why have you done this? I hate this. I hate that you gave this to me and took it all away. I hate that I am so alone now. Why have you done this? Did I deserve this? I will never join ministry again.” Jokes on me, because the Lord put me back in ministry. Then He took it all away again. Instead of asking why this time, and instead of rejecting His calling altogether, I have learned to simply say, “Why do these people hate me? Do they not care if I am walking in the light? Do they even care if I exist?” God seems to have answered that with a resounding “no.” These people don’t care. My work and effort mean nothing to them. They are walking in darkness, that is the truth. God is protecting me from falling in line with them."},{"kind":"para","text":"A real man of God who is really walking in light will not cause any of his brothers to stumble. If he does, he should be willing to see the error and do whatever it takes to fix it before he has lost his brother for good."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"What a funny thought. Darkness can blind the eyes of someone who walks in it. It makes them see everything except for God’s goodness. Furthermore, it makes them blind to what is needful."},{"kind":"para","text":"You can rest assured that a brother who hates you was never actually a brother to begin with. There’s a problem in their faith that they need to address. Perhaps you’ll be the one to get them to see it, but that’s hardly ever the case. You’ll have to wait for the Lord to do His work on them before you can proceed any further in a relationship with them."}]}]},{"start":12,"end":17,"title":"An Eternal Impact","entries":[{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John knows that there is hate happening in the church. They claim themselves as children of light, but they are not treating each other with any sort of love and respect. They are very divided. Perhaps the division was on who they were supposed to love and who they were not. They shunned the sinner to love the saint, or maybe they shunned the saint to love the sinner. Either way, the love of God is supposed to be all-encompassing. It’s supposed to reach out to everyone around you if you are truly living in His love and light. Light does not just touch light; it touches darkness too. If your brother hates you, he’s in darkness, and it’s likely that his light is not reaching anyone because he doesn’t realize that he doesn’t have it on."},{"kind":"para","text":"With that said, Christ has still forgiven them. Even when they are in darkness, Christ gives them forgiveness for their actions and sins if they are willing to accept it. They are acting immature about their walk of faith, however, so John has to write to them as if they are children."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just as the “little children” are not literally John’s children, the fathers in the church are not literally fathers of the church. They are individuals who have been in the faith for a long time and should have gained insights into the walks of faith. Over time, they should have learned and matured with the Lord and life. However, I have known some very old men and women who have walked in the faith for most of their lives yet still live as immature Christians, unable to love others who do not share their beliefs or inner circle."},{"kind":"para","text":"Young men should be taught by the “fathers” of the faith, and older Christians should provide direction and encouragement to younger Christians. Unfortunately, I have never seen this happen in any church in my lifetime. I have tried to mentor many young Christians, but I rarely see others doing the same. People often prefer to associate with those closer to their own age rather than invest in those who are new to the faith. Alternatively, they focus on reaching those without faith rather than helping young Christians grow. John wants to address this issue. He states, “Because you older Christians have known Christ for a while, the younger Christians have some maturing to do, and the older ones should know how to do it and help teach them. The little children will face battles too that the older Christians should prepare them for.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"This is similar to a regular family child. A baby knows literally nothing in this life and must be taught everything, including how to eat. As they grow older, they will face difficult decisions or trials. Because you have faced these before (or simply because you know more now that you are older), you can help them make the right decisions. When they become adults, they will still need help, but they can also give advice to those younger than them and teach others. I do not advise allowing a baby to teach adults. However, that is what the church is doing. New Christians are coming in and are set up to teach older Christians. These children have not learned anything, leading to conflict, disunity, fighting, and a lack of love and light. This is a perfect method for destroying your church."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It’s almost as if John is just repeating himself here. Really, he is. This is as simple as it gets. When you have matured in the faith, the only thing you can do from that point is relive and refresh your faith. How do you do that? You help train the younger Christians. You take care of those that are new to the faith. You don’t just protect them; you teach them. You don’t just throw them into the work either; you go alongside them. You love them as if they truly are your own children."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, I think a lot of pastors need to be looked at in this way. I should not be a father to my pastor. It should be the other way around. I have yet to meet a pastor as such, however."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Going back to what we read earlier, loving not the world does not mean we should hate sinful people. It doesn’t mean we should hate the creation of the Lord. Rather, not loving the world means we should not be attached to it. We should not be intertwined with it. Again, real love is sacrifice. Is there something in this world (aside from your family and friends) that you would sacrifice yourself or someone else for? Perhaps it’s for money or fame. That is what loving the world is. Loving people is far different than loving materials, but without Christ, you wouldn’t be able to tell the difference."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We discussed how there are three themes that John will address: light, love, and life. These three themes will be established and proven by the Lord’s gift to mankind. The world’s gift to mankind is a mockery of those three themes. The lust of the flesh is the world’s version of light, the lust of the eyes is the world’s version of love, and the pride of life is the world’s version of life. God did not ordain these things; the world did. These things control a human being until they come to know the Lord. These three ideals are the world’s commandments. They say, “If you want to make your life brighter, do this drug, or try this method of work.” They pull you in with some sort of “easy money” mentality. They want to numb you to what this world really has to offer so you get used to it and stuck in it. They say, “Marriage is a government idea, and you don’t need it when you can have all the fun you want for free.” They want you to enjoy God’s goodness without doing it in God’s way. They say, “Be proud of who you are, and speak out your own truth. Who cares what everyone else thinks.” Bold. But what they don’t tell you is all the side effects of those actions. They don’t tell you about the diseases, addictions, and loneliness you face through those things. You will find out all of those things after you have been stuck in the enticements of the world for some time. By then, it will be nearly impossible to get out because you are already too deep."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The enticements of the world don’t last forever. They are gone when you are gone. They are gone when the world is gone. The world won’t be around forever. But the Lord will always exist, and any actions you perform for Him and in Him will have an eternal impact. Not just a temporary one."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":21,"title":"Antichrists","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It was the last time the moment that Jesus Christ died. We started living in the last days there. We have strength for the last days, however, through the resurrection of Jesus Christ. While the world continually soaks and spirals itself into darkness, we can still have light in the midst. There were many anti-Christs during the writing of John, but imagine how many more there are today. Let’s not get it confused with the final anti-Christ that is sure to come in time. As our world grows colder and colder to the Lord and truth, it is setting itself up for the perfect false image of God to rule and reign over them. That is another pretty solid reason to not love the things in the world. You might fall for the calling of the anti-Christ, mistaking it for the real Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, a life away from Christ had its moments of joy. I always suffered from depression even before coming to know Christ, but I had some good moments outside of my life with Christ. I think it would have been easy to turn from the Lord and shun His goodness for the rest of my life. The world would have been enticing to me. To numb my pain with drugs and alcohol would have been far easier than living a sober and consistent life for the Lord. Perhaps there are Christians who need medications and help, so I’m not shunning it. However, if we had a stronger church structure of love and light, life would be far easier to live with. Because we don’t, and we have ignored everything that John has written to this church here, we have a lot of people going through their Christian lives alone."},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve had the blessing and privilege to know three different older prayer warriors in my life who have blessed me with their prayers. They had wisdom and encouragement, and I am going to be forever grateful to them and the Lord for it. But I’ve met thousands of older Christians, and I can only account for three who actually cared. That is really sad. I think it was extremely sad to John as well, and the church he was writing to was a “newer” church. The church had just started not that long ago. These were some of the first churches in existence, and they were already starting to disunify and disassociate with love and light. They were not kind to each other right off the bat, and now John has to write about it. I’m thankful this happened though. Otherwise, I would be really confused about what to do when churches today treat their congregants like this."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is even more sad. A lot of these “anti-Christs” came from the church first. They believed the message at first, but then turned from the Lord later on, even rallying people against Him. They want the light and love of Christ without the person of Christ attached to it."},{"kind":"para","text":"Christ’s light and love is truly something to mimic, but it has to be done in the way Christ WANTS it done, otherwise we have the old world’s version of love and light. We already talked about that though."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A true believer can figure out the difference between the wheat and the chaff:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whose fan is in his hand, and he will throughly purge his floor, and gather his wheat into the garner; but he will burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire.","ref":"Matthew 3:12"},{"kind":"para","text":"They can tell the difference. Maybe they will have a hard time separating them, but at the very least they will know which is which and what is real. It’s hard to fake light when you are walking in darkness. It’s like having a lamp with no lightbulb. You know what it is, but it’s useless for its intended purpose."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John feels comfortable writing this letter to them because he knows that they haven’t all drifted away. Again, out of the thousands of older people he has met, there are still three that he can account for being faithful. Out of the thousands of “children” in this church, John can still account for a few that are still faithful to the truth. He wants to talk to them, even though they aren’t many. A few faithful men can make a very large difference in the church. Sadly, they are usually the ones that are overlooked the most as well. John won’t overlook them. He sees them. He cares. He writes to them because he knows what they can do."}]}]},{"start":22,"end":25,"title":"Father And Son","entries":[{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are many ways to identify a liar. The first and most obvious way is if they ascribe to Jesus being the Christ. If they do not believe Jesus is the Savior of mankind, they are considered an antichrist. This verse is particularly interesting because John states that they must believe in both the Father and the Son. They are synonymous. Some people only believe in the Father. Muslims claim to believe in the Father, calling Him Allah, but they do not believe in the Son. They deny that Jesus is the Christ. Mormons, Jehovah’s Witnesses, and even certain “Christian” groups hold this view. It baffles me. It truly does. But if they cannot agree to that one simple truth, you can determine them to be untrustworthy of anything else they say. It doesn’t matter their political thinking, their degrees or prefixes, or how smart they might be in this area or that area. If they do not believe Jesus is the Christ, everything else they think and believe goes out the window. Some good people will say good things, but they cannot be trusted without having faith in Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"With that said, I have taken a lot of advice and interesting facts from interesting people who do not believe in the Lord. I will take them to heart, but I will compare them to scripture before I trust them. It boils down to God’s Word being final, not man’s."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Just like you can’t have light without love (and vice-versa), you can’t have the Father without the Son (and vice-versa). They belong together. They are one. You can’t deny the Son and still believe the Father. You can’t deny the Father and still believe the Son. You must have both. Then the Holy Spirit is involved with this as well. But again, He is one with the Father and Son."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Wherefore I say unto you, All manner of sin and blasphemy shall be forgiven unto men: but the blasphemy against the Holy Ghost shall not be forgiven unto men.","ref":"Matthew 12:31"},{"kind":"para","text":"The same consequences apply for denying the Father and Son. We will talk more about this in chapter 5."}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Faith in the Lord as Father and Son is what every child of God should be living in. We should abide in that knowledge until the day of our death. A lot of people have given up that truth, not because it is no longer relevant. Some people, I believe, have given up the truth of this because they are too tired to keep holding faith. Their faith wasn’t resting upon the Lord. It was, perhaps, resting upon the church to do what was right by the Lord. I believe that is why a lot of people left the church that John was writing to, to become anti-Christs. Their faith rested upon the people of the church to be good godly examples, and instead, they started mistreating their own people. So they thought it was all a fraud, and now they are fighting against it rather than joining it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think there needs to be people fighting against the falsities of faith in our modern churches today. But that doesn’t mean you throw out the truth with it. These people don’t like something in the church, and instead of learning the truth by their own knowledge and searching, they just reach for something else that can occupy them. They want someone smarter than them to give them the truth. Usually, those people will give them something, but if they don’t believe Jesus is the Christ (as I’ve said before), they will likely not give you much truth. Just a bunch of ideas and theories that usually go against Christ. That is when these people become “anti-Christs” and don’t just belittle the terrible leaders of the church, but also try and ruin the minds of the young “children” in the faith. They try and pull these new Christians away from the truth so that they don’t become like these old, calloused, evil leaders in the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"There probably are a lot of old calloused leaders. Thankfully, John was not one of them. So he’s sending a letter of love and light to help these wayward Christians to see the truth is still the truth, whether there are people who want to follow it or not."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For what if some did not believe? shall their unbelief make the faith of God without effect? God forbid: yea, let God be true, but every man a liar; as it is written, That thou mightest be justified in thy sayings, and mightest overcome when thou art judged.","ref":"Romans 3:3-4"}]},{"start":25,"end":25,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Because we have the truth and live in the truth of God’s Word, we have a promise of eternal life. Don’t let your faith rest upon spiritual leaders; let it only rest upon Jesus Christ."},{"kind":"para","text":"Going to church and reaching the lost is all good and great, but that is not what your faith rests on. Working in ministry and feeding the homeless is all good and great, but that is not what your faith rests on. Learning new things and digging into theories is all good and great, but that is not what your faith rests on. If your faith does not rest on the finished work of Jesus Christ alone, you will continually walk in darkness until it does."}]}]},{"start":26,"end":29,"title":"Seducing Spirits","entries":[{"start":26,"end":26,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I find it fascinating that when people fall away from the faith, they often try their best to pull others away from it too. They say it’s out of love, but I don’t think it is. I’ve left a couple of churches in my time, but I have never tried to entice others to follow me. In fact, when I left Texas, the teens asked me what they should do. I told them to fight the fight I could not win. They said, “I don’t want to stay in this church anymore.” I replied, “I don’t encourage you to leave. I would rather you stay and make a difference that I could not make.” They asked, “What if we can’t make a difference?” I said, “Then find a church where you can, but don’t leave this one until you do.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve never told people to walk away from the faith. I never encouraged the people I love to leave their church homes and become homeless because of me. I feel very confident being an encourager rather than a discourager. I want to encourage people to do the things they love if it’s healthy for them. The faith and the church are not always places of physical wealth, but they should be places of spiritual health. That’s not always the case, but I can pray for change and hope. We should never be people pulling others away from truth because we don’t like something. We should only be looking to keep people in the truth, even if we are hurt by them."}]},{"start":27,"end":27,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Those people who have left the faith were not anointed by the truth of Christ. They wouldn’t be able to leave the truth if they were truly anointed. It would live with them forever. They might not be in the church, but they cannot leave the faith once they have the Holy Spirit. If they can successfully leave the faith, they are either lying or they never had faith to begin with."},{"kind":"para","text":"That might seem too Calvinist for some of you, but that is simply the truth. You can’t hold something and then say you no longer believe it existed. I can’t put an orange in your hand and you tell me that you don’t believe in oranges now. That would just be silly. Either that or you don’t actually know what an orange is."}]},{"start":28,"end":28,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Living in Christ is the greatest gift you can give to God, and it is also the greatest gift the Lord can give to you. Knowing for sure what pleases God and what doesn’t is a good way to have assurance in everything you do. This helps you make decisions easily. Not only will this make you happy and the Lord happy, but it will also make everyone else around you happy because you can lead them with confidence."}]},{"start":29,"end":29,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another great thing to live in is the belief in God’s righteousness. Sometimes, we are faced with questions or situations where we question whether or not God is really righteous. Why would He allow something like this to happen? Is He being righteous even in the trials and hurts of this world?"},{"kind":"para","text":"God is always righteous, and that is something we must respect and accept if we are going to have confidence in our brothers in Christ."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to every one that believeth.","ref":"Romans 10:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Being filled with the fruits of righteousness, which are by Jesus Christ, unto the glory and praise of God.","ref":"Philippians 1:11"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD is righteous in all his ways, and holy in all his works.","ref":"Psalm 145:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"God is righteous. When we see terrible things happening in this world, we don’t have to question if He is still being righteous. Terrible things happen by sinners, while right things happen by the Lord. God always makes things right. We don’t have to question whether or not God is righteous; we just have to question whether or not others are righteous by Him. If people are not being righteous, it is only because they are in darkness, not because God is not righteous. People will only do righteous things if they have Jesus as their Christ. They will know how to walk in the light because He has shown them how to do it. We will walk with Him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"He must increase, but I must decrease.","ref":"John 3:30"}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24},{"start":25,"end":25},{"start":26,"end":26},{"start":27,"end":27},{"start":28,"end":28},{"start":29,"end":29}],"verses":{"1":"My little children, these things write I unto you, that ye sin not. And if any man sin, we have an advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous:","2":"And he is the propitiation for our sins: and not for ours only, but also for the sins of the whole world.","3":"And hereby we do know that we know him, if we keep his commandments.","4":"He that saith, I know him, and keepeth not his commandments, is a liar, and the truth is not in him.","5":"But whoso keepeth his word, in him verily is the love of God perfected: hereby know we that we are in him.","6":"He that saith he abideth in him ought himself also so to walk, even as he walked.","7":"Brethren, I write no new commandment unto you, but an old commandment which ye had from the beginning. The old commandment is the word which ye have heard from the beginning.","8":"Again, a new commandment I write unto you, which thing is true in him and in you: because the darkness is past, and the true light now shineth.","9":"He that saith he is in the light, and hateth his brother, is in darkness even until now.","10":"He that loveth his brother abideth in the light, and there is none occasion of stumbling in him.","11":"But he that hateth his brother is in darkness, and walketh in darkness, and knoweth not whither he goeth, because that darkness hath blinded his eyes.","12":"I write unto you, little children, because your sins are forgiven you for his name's sake.","13":"I write unto you, fathers, because ye have known him that is from the beginning. I write unto you, young men, because ye have overcome the wicked one. I write unto you, little children, because ye have known the Father.","14":"I have written unto you, fathers, because ye have known him that is from the beginning. I have written unto you, young men, because ye are strong, and the word of God abideth in you, and ye have overcome the wicked one.","15":"Love not the world, neither the things that are in the world. If any man love the world, the love of the Father is not in him.","16":"For all that is in the world, the lust of the flesh, and the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life, is not of the Father, but is of the world.","17":"And the world passeth away, and the lust thereof: but he that doeth the will of God abideth for ever.","18":"Little children, it is the last time: and as ye have heard that antichrist shall come, even now are there many antichrists; whereby we know that it is the last time.","19":"They went out from us, but they were not of us; for if they had been of us, they would no doubt have continued with us: but they went out, that they might be made manifest that they were not all of us.","20":"But ye have an unction from the Holy One, and ye know all things.","21":"I have not written unto you because ye know not the truth, but because ye know it, and that no lie is of the truth.","22":"Who is a liar but he that denieth that Jesus is the Christ? He is antichrist, that denieth the Father and the Son.","23":"Whosoever denieth the Son, the same hath not the Father: he that acknowledgeth the Son hath the Father also.","24":"Let that therefore abide in you, which ye have heard from the beginning. If that which ye have heard from the beginning shall remain in you, ye also shall continue in the Son, and in the Father.","25":"And this is the promise that he hath promised us, even eternal life.","26":"These things have I written unto you concerning them that seduce you.","27":"But the anointing which ye have received of him abideth in you, and ye need not that any man teach you: but as the same anointing teacheth you of all things, and is truth, and is no lie, and even as it hath taught you, ye shall abide in him.","28":"And now, little children, abide in him; that, when he shall appear, we may have confidence, and not be ashamed before him at his coming.","29":"If ye know that he is righteous, ye know that every one that doeth righteousness is born of him."}},
    "3": {"chapter":3,"intro":[],"previously":["In our last chapter, John emphasized the importance of living a faith that aligns with the Lord’s example, rather than merely professing faith without action. He highlighted the love we should have for those within our faith community. John addressed the issue of hating one’s brother in the faith and the significant impact a united community can have. He also discussed how far the world has strayed from the Lord, even referring to them as “antichrists,” as they are indeed against Christ. John affirmed that believers cannot simply believe in God’s existence without acknowledging that Jesus gave His life and IS God. In this chapter, John continues the theme of love, discussing how the children of God should conduct themselves towards one another in the faith. While it may be challenging to love people in the world, we should not find it difficult to love those within our faith community. However, this remains a significant issue in John’s time and continues to be a prominent problem in modern churches today."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Children of God","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we haven’t recognized it yet, the main theme that John wants to stress in these books is the main theme he has personally learned to stress in his own life. That theme is love. The love of God should be enough to change the children of God. Honestly, it’s what makes us become children of God. We don’t have God’s love; we don’t have God’s grace. Without His grace, we are doomed to an eternity away from the Lord. In fact, those who don’t know the Lord don’t understand you or your love for the Lord. They can’t. They simply won’t. They don’t want to."},{"kind":"para","text":"I was talking to a guy the other day about this exact thing, and he was very adamant in telling me that God does not exist and there is no forgiveness for me. What was funny to me was his choice of wording. He said, “I’m sorry to have to tell you this…” and proceeded to try and put me down with vulgarity and lies rejecting the validity of God’s grace, love, and truth. The fact that he said, “I’m sorry” was the funny part to me. Does he want me to forgive his vulgarity? What a strange concept that he would apologize seeing as he personally believes that I won’t be forgiven for my own shortcomings, yet he expects me to forgive him for his. The thing is, I do forgive him. I forgive him not only because he’s a stranger but because I know he needs forgiveness. I am quite positive he has never experienced forgiveness before. He said a lot of awful things to me (things that Satan tells me all the time), but that honestly didn’t bother me as much as it made me feel sad that he thought he had to treat people like that. He has no joy in himself, and he was trying to rob others of it too."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, that is the hot take for most pastors and congregants today. They don’t have joy in what they are doing. Instead of trying to produce joy or be around people who can help increase their joy, they try to rob everyone else of the joy of the Lord. If we truly know the love of the Lord, then we should be producing joy in others, not robbing them of it. If you find a man who is robbing people of joy or giving people a reason or excuse to not have joy, then that person doesn’t know God’s love (according to this book of the Bible). Perhaps we shouldn’t just take John’s word for it. Perhaps we should look to Jesus Christ alone about this matter of love and joy."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A new commandment I give unto you, That ye love one another; as I have loved you, that ye also love one another. By this shall all men know that ye are my disciples, if ye have love one to another.","ref":"John 13:34-35"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Greater love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends.","ref":"John 15:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"It's interchangeable with the word \"charity\" in scripture:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Charity suffereth long, and is kind; charity envieth not; charity vaunteth not itself, is not puffed up, Doth not behave itself unseemly, seeketh not her own, is not easily provoked, thinketh no evil; Rejoiceth not in iniquity, but rejoiceth in the truth; Beareth all things, believeth all things, hopeth all things, endureth all things. Charity never faileth: but whether there be prophecies, they shall fail; whether there be tongues, they shall cease; whether there be knowledge, it shall vanish away.","ref":"1 Corinthians 13:4-8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let all your things be done with charity.","ref":"1 Corinthians 16:14"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And above all these things put on charity, which is the bond of perfectness.","ref":"Colossians 3:14"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And above all things have fervent charity among yourselves: for charity shall cover the multitude of sins.","ref":"1 Peter 4:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"My brethren, count it all joy when ye fall into divers temptations;","ref":"James 1:2"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Rejoice in the Lord alway: and again I say, Rejoice.","ref":"Philippians 4:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Hitherto have ye asked nothing in my name: ask, and ye shall receive, that your joy may be full.","ref":"John 16:24"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Whom having not seen, ye love; in whom, though now ye see him not, yet believing, ye rejoice with joy unspeakable and full of glory:","ref":"1 Peter 1:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"The point is, joy and love are not exclusive; they belong together. If you love something, you will have joy in that thing. If you have joy in something, it is because you love it. The Lord loves us, and when we understand that, it produces joy in our lives that just doesn’t make sense. Even people who are ugly or mean to you cannot take that joy because God’s love has been proven to you. Their mean gestures are no match for the death and pain of the cross; in fact, they are deflected."},{"kind":"para","text":"I used to think the same way honestly. There is no forgiveness for this world. There is no hope, no chance, no purpose. But now that I know the love of God, it brings me great joy in knowing that there is a chance for people, and that people can truly change if they are willing to allow the Lord to change them. That is where lasting change comes from. That is what lasting joy looks like."},{"kind":"para","text":"The part that might seem somewhat confusing to some is the “that we should be called the sons of God…” remark. Thankfully, John clarifies deeper:"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are a couple of revelations here from John. First, John insinuates something we should already know: when we turn to the Lord, He is not just a judge; He is a Father:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But to us there is but one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we in him; and one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him.","ref":"1 Corinthians 8:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"One God and Father of all, who is above all, and through all, and in you all.","ref":"Ephesians 4:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And call no man your father upon the earth: for one is your Father, which is in heaven.","ref":"Matthew 23:9"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"A father of the fatherless, and a judge of the widows, is God in his holy habitation.","ref":"Psalms 68:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But now, O LORD, thou art our father; we are the clay, and thou our potter; and we all are the work of thy hand.","ref":"Isaiah 64:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, and cometh down from the Father of lights, with whom is no variableness, neither shadow of turning.","ref":"James 1:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think we get the point. God is a Father to His children, and we are not His children until we turn to His gift of salvation. There are a lot of people who might claim to be a child of God, but they have no association with Jesus Christ. If they reject Christ and the Spirit, they don’t have God as their Father. They have God as their judge. There is no bigger worry in this world than to be without the mercy of God on the day of judgment."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A man who turns to the Lord learns to purify himself to be more like the Lord. When explaining to another man about God’s wrath and mercy, one of the things he kept coming back to was that God’s wrath was not real. He kept claiming that people are evil and then telling me that I should listen to him about all of these advices. He was belittling me and being cruel, yet he wanted me to take his advice? All I could tell him was, “You know, maybe the Lord isn’t for you? But I sure need Him. I’ve done some terrible things in this life, and I am glad that He has not sent judgment before saving me. I need His mercy and grace. I, personally, am thankful for His gift of a second chance at life. I may not atone, but I can at least strive to live by His example.” Honestly, that is more than I can say for this other guy, no offense to him. He was just like the world in almost every way possible. How miserable the world looks when you have the eyes of faith to produce joy in you."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we have Christ, we don’t just have pure motives and pure intentions. We are purified in every facet of life and business. We have the ability to grow and learn to do better than we ever could before. He’s given us a pure work in our lives and walks, and the world will never understand that because they want to be more miserable with the world. They hate the world but don’t seek to change their own lives to be less like the world. Honestly, it baffles my mind."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I was an agnostic, I didn’t try to be more like the world. I hated the world back then. Instead of complaining about how much the world sucked, I did something about it. I was an ear to quite a bit of people, kind of like a school therapist. I had so many people coming to me and talking to me because I knew what it was like to feel what they felt. I knew how to hurt with people. Instead of letting the world drag me down with it, I tried to be a shoulder for people who were hurting. I don’t see this happen much anymore, not even in the church."},{"kind":"para","text":"The world complains about the world and then does nothing to help humanity. The church complains about the world and then does nothing to help humanity. It seems that the world and the church are starting to blur together in a lot of places…"}]}]},{"start":4,"end":8,"title":"Unfading Hope","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This single verse contains a lot of doctrine and serves as proof of quite a few things. First, it demonstrates that breaking God’s law is something that every person is guilty of. Sin is the transgression of the law. You cannot sin without breaking God’s law. Sinning literally means you have broken His law, at least according to John."},{"kind":"para","text":"What is considered sin then? We have a few definitions for it (just to cover our bases) throughout scripture:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Therefore to him that knoweth to do good, and doeth it not, to him it is sin.","ref":"James 4:17"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then when lust hath conceived, it bringeth forth sin: and sin, when it is finished, bringeth forth death.","ref":"James 1:15"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"All unrighteousness is sin: and there is a sin not unto death.","ref":"1 John 5:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"John states that sin is the transgression of the law. Essentially, sin is falling short of God’s righteousness. It is not being able to compare to the standards of God’s holiness. We cannot. Therefore, when we come into this world, we are sinful. Even if we had never done something wrong, we are still sinful beings. My seven-month-old daughter is a sinner. No, she will not go to Hell for her sins (she doesn’t understand forgiveness, judgment, repentance, or salvation yet), but she was born in sin."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, I was shapen in iniquity; and in sin did my mother conceive me.","ref":"Psalm 51:5"},{"kind":"para","text":"Sin is in our DNA from birth due to coming from the lineage of sin. We are all born sinners. However, the Lord gives us freedom and forgiveness from that sin."},{"kind":"para","text":"The person I was talking to about God’s wrath and mercy kept saying that I will not be forgiven. He repeated this several times, emphasizing that when the time comes, God is not going to forgive me. He was missing one key ingredient, though, when he was trying to convince me (and it’s one that Satan misses quite often as well). That ingredient is: God has already forgiven me. I don’t have to wait for Him to do it. He proved it on the cross. The only way that this person could convince me that God has not forgiven me is to prove to me that Jesus did not come and die on the cross for my sins, rising from the dead three days later. Seeing as that is impossible to prove, I stand in strong faith that the Lord has kept His promise on this matter, just as He has kept His promise on all other matters. On top of this, the Spirit of God proves the forgiveness of God."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the Comforter, which is the Holy Ghost, whom the Father will send in my name, he shall teach you all things, and bring all things to your remembrance, whatsoever I have said unto you.","ref":"John 14:26"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the law of the Spirit of life in Christ Jesus hath made me free from the law of sin and death.","ref":"Romans 8:2"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then Peter said unto them, Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins, and ye shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost.","ref":"Acts 2:38"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me; because the LORD hath anointed me to preach good tidings unto the meek; he hath sent me to bind up the brokenhearted, to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to them that are bound;","ref":"Isaiah 61:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Howbeit when he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will guide you into all truth: for he shall not speak of himself; but whatsoever he shall hear, that shall he speak: and he will shew you things to come.","ref":"John 16:13"},{"kind":"para","text":"I do apologize if this chapter seems scripture heavy, but these are important truths in our era today. We all need this ammunition because the enemy has been in constant work to try and convince you that God does not love you, and there is no hope for you or His people. That simply isn’t true. At least not according to truth, and God’s Word."},{"kind":"para","text":"As a personally changed man, I can attest that God has forgiven mankind through the sacrifice of His Son, and anyone that turns to Him in true belief and repentance will receive the gift of life. Not just in eternity, but for RIGHT NOW. Notice that John has not said, “I am writing these things so that your joy may be full… in eternity.” Rather, John says that your joy can be full right now! He wants you to CURRENTLY live in joy. It’s possible to do. It’s difficult when the world is trying to blind you from it, but the Lord’s actions, Spirit, and truth should not be able to be blocked by the lies of this world."},{"kind":"para","text":"As I told the man the other day, “I’d rather take the truth of God’s word that He loves me, than to take the lies of man that He doesn’t.” Every person is able to live in the truth. It’s an open invitation to everyone."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The truth is, the reason Jesus came was not just to give us an example. He certainly did provide a great example to humanity, but that was not all He was here for. He was not here just to heal the sick. He was not here just to minister to the needy. He was not even here just to preach the gospel to the lost. He was here to die a painfully brutal death and then prove His salvation to the world by resurrecting and showing Himself to over five hundred people. He did all of that according to scripture, and according to His Spirit, He changes the lives of people every single day."},{"kind":"para","text":"It would be one thing if I was just blindly following the Lord without question, but I’m not. In fact, real faith tests everything. Someone said, “If it can’t be tested, it can’t be trusted.” I’ve seen this life change in many people’s lives. I’ve seen it in my own, in my wife, in my daughter, in friends, in family, and in strangers. God’s salvation absolutely changes people, and there is no way to deny it other than to just completely ignore the proof right in front of your face."},{"kind":"para","text":"People today are good at ignoring what is right in front of them. Not because they don’t want to believe, but only because they don’t want to admit they are wrong. They are prideful, and that is what their legacy will leave behind when they are gone. They won’t leave behind value; they will leave behind staleness and bitterness. But when we have Jesus, we have assurance of forgiveness of our sins, because we are promised (according to scripture) that He died to cover our sins."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For Christ also hath once suffered for sins, the just for the unjust, that he might bring us to God, being put to death in the flesh, but quickened by the Spirit:","ref":"1 Peter 3:18"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed.","ref":"Isaiah 53:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"When Jesus therefore had received the vinegar, he said, It is finished: and he bowed his head, and gave up the ghost.","ref":"John 19:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"What is finished? The payment for our sins is finished."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Verily, verily, I say unto you, He that heareth my word, and believeth on him that sent me, hath everlasting life, and shall not come into condemnation; but is passed from death unto life.","ref":"John 5:24"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Blotting out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us, which was contrary to us, and took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross;","ref":"Colossians 2:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"Do you see how obvious scripture makes it to the reader that Jesus Christ’s mission was to save people from their sins through His suffering on the cross? What an unworthy gift we have all received."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have an interesting verse here. John has already told us that everyone is a sinner, and anyone who says they are not sinful is a liar. Now he says, “If you are a sinner, you don’t know Him.” There is careful consideration to his words here that we need to understand. It’s not that we are not sinners, but we are not unrepentant sinners. If we don’t repent from our evil actions, we are simple sinners. We are sinful on purpose, and there is no excuse for our evil actions. If we willfully sin, we are not truly saved by the Lord. Sin should be a struggle to the believer. If you are not struggling with sin, you are either a liar or a phony."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"And this world will try to \"deceive\" you into thinking that God is not righteous."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to every one that believeth.","ref":"Romans 10:4"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Flee also youthful lusts: but follow righteousness, faith, charity, peace, with them that call on the Lord out of a pure heart.","ref":"2 Timothy 2:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"When we know the righteousness of God, it is a very difficult thing to reject it in our own lives. Those who can reject God’s righteousness are not righteous people. They will lie, rob, hurt, and destroy you at the drop of a hat. They don’t care about you at all. If they are busy trying to convince you that you don’t matter and you don’t belong, those are people who don’t know the righteousness of God. They are not righteous either."},{"kind":"para","text":"Therefore, it’s best not to take their words and thoughts to heart because they don’t know what righteousness even is. Take God’s Word about it alone, even if it seems to contradict everything everyone else is saying. If God says, (at least from what I’ve seen in my own personal life), it is absolutely true and testable."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Satan is a fallen angel, created by God for worship. We have discussed this a few times before. The problem was that Satan wanted to hoard the worship for himself. God cast him out of heaven, and Satan took other angels with him, who are now referred to as demons. Because Satan cannot overthrow God, his next goal was to try and trick God’s creation to follow him rather than God. Little did Satan know that he was actually falling for God’s design this whole time. God wanted a deeper relationship with His humanity, and now we have that through faith, and in time through sight."},{"kind":"para","text":"To say I love my wife without meeting her would be a far stretch. To say that I love anyone that I’ve never met wouldn’t be very accurate. Yet, my love for the Lord is deeper than my love for my wife. I love my wife because of sight first, then spirit second. I love the Lord because of spirit first, then sight second. I can’t see the Lord, not yet anyway, but I look forward to the day that I get to."},{"kind":"para","text":"Satan’s desire is to keep as many people as possible from seeing the face of God or having an impactful relationship with God. Boy, is he good at it. He uses the same tactics on everyone, though. It’s easy to see his tricks at this point. He just uses the world to blind people. Everyone without God is stuck in a useless cycle of materialism to try and fill a void that will never be full with anything in this life. It lasts for a short time, but then they have to move on to the next thing, like drugs. Everyone in the world is trying to find a lasting high so they don’t have to deal with their own sinful deeds and can ignore the world around them better. You won’t find anything lasting on this Earth. That is why we must have the Son. He has died, but He continues to live forever. Because He came back to life, He has given us the ability to put our hope in something that cannot fade."}]}]},{"start":9,"end":12,"title":"Sinners Defined","entries":[{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Being “born of God” is the second chance life of a person. We are born into this world in the flesh, but we are born of God in the Spirit."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Marvel not that I said unto thee, Ye must be born again.","ref":"John 3:7"},{"kind":"para","text":"There is a difference between falling into sin and living in sin. A man may be saved and fall into sin, but that doesn’t mean he is not born of God. Rather, it means he still has a fleshly nature, which John discussed as being the case for everyone in the chapters before this. Instead, what John is saying here is that if you are born of God, you will not live in sin. You won’t sin on purpose, and you won’t reject the Lord in your sin. You might fall into sin, but you know it is a sin and you know that it needs to be forgiven."},{"kind":"para","text":"A man who is not born of God will not seek to have his sins forgiven. He doesn’t mind his sin. He doesn’t mind hurting people or God. In fact, he will do it on purpose to make himself feel better about his own shortcomings. If someone is trying to put you down for your faith, you can bet any money that it’s only because they are insecure about their own sin. They will try to put you down because they want to make their sin feel normal. They don’t want to change their evil ways. They want to remain evil and cruel, so in order to feel good about their evil deeds, they try to tear your faith apart so you are just like them."},{"kind":"para","text":"What they don’t realize is that I am just like them. I am a sinner. The only difference between me and any other sinner is that I have a purpose to reject sin because of the forgiveness of Jesus Christ. I don’t have to hurt people to make myself feel better about my sin. I can just go to God and ask for forgiveness for my shortcomings and direction about changing my heart. He always provides an answer to that in His Word. Obeying it is the Christian’s duty. Disobeying the Lord is the world’s duty."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is an interesting verse, but it describes so much of why John wrote this letter. It isn’t simply about loving God and living for Him. That is certainly part of it. It’s about unifying the people of the church. That is something we have spoken about several times already, but John is reminding the reader of that important fact here. Loving God and loving the saints must relate to each other. You cannot say you love God and then treat your brother in the faith like trash. You can’t have one without the other."},{"kind":"para","text":"It should also be a no-brainer to the reader that a person who does not act righteously is not a man of God. Sometimes we misunderstand this, though. We are shocked to find many people who are living in the world and are not acting righteously. We shouldn’t be surprised when a pig rolls in the mud. We shouldn’t be shocked to find out that dogs bark. It isn’t new knowledge to learn that frogs jump. Therefore, it should not blindside us when unrighteous people don’t act righteously."},{"kind":"para","text":"Furthermore, that is how a man of God tests a person of God. If they say they are God’s children, yet they are not righteous in their conduct, you can be sure that the person in question is not a child of God. Somehow, we will still be surprised to learn that unrighteous people will do unrighteous things…"}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This world will not love one another. If you don’t have the faith of the Lord, you might love your own for a short time, but eventually, you will stab someone in the back or be stabbed in the back. The world is not good at looking out for its own. Christians should have the better advantage here because we have reason and an example of how real unity should look. The church should be a beacon of unity in this world. Sadly, it hardly ever is today."},{"kind":"para","text":"The world and the faith have blurred lines now, and it is very difficult to tell them apart. But the true message of faith is unity and love with the saints. That is what real faith looks like. We should be so full of the love of God that it just automatically flows out into others around us (saved or not)."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Darkness and light can't exist together."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Be ye not unequally yoked together with unbelievers: for what fellowship hath righteousness with unrighteousness? and what communion hath light with darkness?","ref":"2 Corinthians 6:14"},{"kind":"para","text":"I like how John uses Cain and Abel here. It is the perfect illustration of the world versus faith. The world wants to come out on top. A faith-filled person just wants to come out, whether that’s in the mud, a ditch, or a mountain, it doesn’t matter. We just want to get through and grow closer to God. Not the world, though. If the world can’t get out on top, they will kill people to make sure it happens. Cain killed his own brother so that he could come out on top. He killed his own brother because he wanted to be the “better” one. Really, it all just boils down to pride and bitterness. The world dwells in pride and bitterness. A man of God should not. Many “children of God” are bitter and prideful, however, and they become no different than Cain. Arguably, that is likely their lineage account. We either come from the lineage of Cain or the lineage of Seth. Cain is the lineage of sin. Seth is the lineage that was set apart. We can be adopted into the lineage of Seth if we choose to accept the adoption of the Lord through His Son’s sacrifice:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For as many as are led by the Spirit of God, they are the sons of God. For ye have not received the spirit of bondage again to fear; but ye have received the Spirit of adoption, whereby we cry, Abba, Father. The Spirit itself beareth witness with our spirit, that we are the children of God:","ref":"Romans 8:14-16"},{"kind":"para","text":"Being in the Lord’s lineage is truly what impacts a person to do what’s right by others. If you do not belong to the Lord, you will have no problem killing your brother over nothing simply because you are jealous of him. Whether spiritually or physically, men of this world will hurt even the closest people to them. That will not happen with men of God (if they truly are men of God)."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":18,"title":"Deed and Truth","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another thing that should not surprise us is that strangers hate me because I have faith in Jesus. I literally don’t even have to talk to them, and they will still hate me. They don’t believe in Jesus, but they hate me for believing it. It is quite strange, but it shouldn’t be a surprise."},{"kind":"para","text":"John says, “Don’t let that even phase you. Don’t ‘marvel’ at it. It’s not worth your time.” It shouldn’t surprise us because it’s something that has happened since the beginning of time."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are some core truths that John is going to reveal in this section of scripture. These are truths that aren’t often discussed in the church today. I hear great and mighty messages about being a soldier for the Lord, messages about keeping the faith and standing firm on convictions, and intense messages of repentance, change, and work in the ministry. I’ve had life-changing messages thrown at me about doing this thing or that thing for the Lord, and even messages about being a grand soulwinner and public speaker."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, I’ve never—not one time since I’ve been saved for the last eleven years as of this writing—heard a message about loving your brothers in the church and keeping unity. Not one. I’ve never heard a unifying message from the book of 1 John. Honestly, the only time I’ve heard a preacher preach from 1 John was just to address the need to have joy in your work for the Lord. They missed the whole purpose of this letter. It wasn’t just about having joy; it was about having unity in the work. We should be working ministry together. Ministry work wasn’t made for man to do alone. In fact, there is no work in this world that man should be doing alone. God doesn’t like it when man is alone, remember? He wants everything to be done in unity."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Fulfil ye my joy, that ye be likeminded, having the same love, being of one accord, of one mind.","ref":"Philippians 2:2"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Finally, be ye all of one mind, having compassion one of another, love as brethren, be pitiful, be courteous:","ref":"1 Peter 3:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Now I beseech you, brethren, by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that ye all speak the same thing, and that there be no divisions among you; but that ye be perfectly joined together in the same mind and in the same judgment.","ref":"1 Corinthians 1:10"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I therefore, the prisoner of the Lord, beseech you that ye walk worthy of the vocation wherewith ye are called, With all lowliness and meekness, with longsuffering, forbearing one another in love; Endeavouring to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace. There is one body, and one Spirit, even as ye are called in one hope of your calling; One Lord, one faith, one baptism, One God and Father of all, who is above all, and through all, and in you all.","ref":"Ephesians 4:1-6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Behold, how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity!","ref":"Psalm 133:1"},{"kind":"para","text":"There are so many others I could use. If you miss the message of unity, you are not reading the Bible. That has been the main theme since the beginning of scripture. Adam didn’t sin alone either; he had Eve with him. Cain sinned by murdering his brother, and there was no unity. Noah had his family while building the ark. Even Jacob had his family while he was coming back home."},{"kind":"para","text":"Without unity in the faith, we have people thrown into pits, sold into slavery, beheaded for the truth, fed to lions, bludgeoned to death by bats and stones, robbed of power, position, and purpose, loneliness, confusion, strife, envy… The list goes on. Without unity, you have the world. The only people that can have real unity are those that are really full of the faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"If there is no unity in a church, the leader’s faith is not full. His joy is not full. His truth is not full. His life is not full. His grace is not full. His love is not full. Perhaps they are full, but they are not full of those things. They are full of self. In order to be filled by the Lord, you cannot be full of yourself. In order to love your brothers correctly, you cannot be full of yourself. In order to be faithful, you cannot be full of yourself."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Let nothing be done through strife or vainglory; but in lowliness of mind let each esteem other better than themselves. Look not every man on his own things, but every man also on the things of others.","ref":"Philippians 2:3-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Faith-filled people are not self-filled people. They look to fill the faith of those around them, not rob them of what they have."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The truth is, the world is full of hateful people. I think it’s fine to hate certain things, but I don’t think it’s fine to hate “people” in particular. Though there are some people who you have no choice but to hate. I’m going to be honest… I hate California’s governor. He’s more subtle than Hitler, but he does some terrible things. Justifying the murder of babies is worse (in my opinion) than justifying the murder of adults. But this man, Gavin, kills, robs, and betrays his own people all in the name of greed and profit. Then he lies to his own people about it and tells them that he will take care of them. Honestly, there are a lot of pastors who do this same thing. I don’t think you can trust a politician today, no matter what side they might land on. But I have no sympathy for a man that doesn’t see what’s wrong with killing unborn babies. I draw the line of peace at hurting children. Come after me all you want; if you come after my kids, I have every right (at that point) to play your game unfairly, and retaliation is final."},{"kind":"para","text":"With that said, John says here that we should not be hating our “brothers.” Who are our brothers? This could be defined as our literal blood-related brothers. I’m sure it is fine to interpret this verse as such, but I believe it is speaking more about spiritual brothers. John calls the church his “children” when they are not literally his blood children. He calls them the Lord’s children. So everything is taken on a spiritual level, which would mean that his verse is included in that. These are brothers of the faith. You might say, “Why would I hate my brother in Christ?” Maybe you don’t. That’s great! But they might hate you. I see a lot of division in the church today. I’m not saying it to bash the church; I’m saying it because that is the truth. The church is full of hate. I’ve seen churches that absolutely hate people on the outside of it, but I’ve seen churches hate the people on the inside far more. How does that happen? Well, someone’s faith is off. They claim to love God, but they hate their brother. That is why John must say this here. It’s not a new concept for our modern churches."},{"kind":"para","text":"What really annoys me is that it has been written for us to learn by, and yet the church seems to ignore this message altogether today. To hate your brother in the Lord is the equivalent of being a murderer. You may not kill the physical being of a person, but you will certainly kill their faith and hope. What a terrible person you must be to do that to your congregants. The truth is, if you are okay with doing that, or you have done that and have not made it right, you are living as a murderer and a fraud."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A faithful man lays his life down for others, especially for those of the faith. I have real friends in the faith—people who aren’t tainted by the pride of power or the call of money. I’ve learned that ministry men make terrible friends. I don’t know what it is, but I’ve seen the money and comfort of the ministry seriously tear people apart and rob them of real connection. Everyone becomes a prospect rather than a soul. Friends don’t treat friends like prospects. You don’t treat your brother as a means to an end. You love the saints because the Lord loves you. That is it. That is the only reason to pour anything out to others; it’s simply because God has poured it out to you. If God has done it for you, it only makes sense that you would be willing to do it for others."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Good question, John. I don’t think a man who is willing to shun other men of the faith really has the love of God in them. I have plenty of things to say about this, but it’s best reserved for a physical conversation rather than writing about it here."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I’ve said this before, but it’s worth repeating: I can’t just tell my wife I love her and not show it. In the same sense, a pastor cannot tell his congregants and co-laborers that he loves them and then not show it. Saying “I love you” means nothing if I don’t show it through physical conversation and connection. It doesn’t have to be anything big either. It just has to be genuine."},{"kind":"para","text":"People can tell what is genuine love and care and what is not. Some pastors are pretty good at faking their care, but no pastor is good at faking love. I can tell a liar from a mile away, and a man of God should not be found as liars for compassion and comfort."}]}]},{"start":19,"end":24,"title":"A Right Standing","entries":[{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You cannot fake love towards others and then expect God to appease you. You cannot fake your faith and then assume God will back you up. You cannot simply say, “But Lord, I told him that I loved him, he still didn’t believe me, that’s HIS problem not mine,” and expect the Lord to just ignore your lack of effort to show real love towards your brethren. No true man of God should sit comfortably before the Lord after lying about his value and care for others."},{"kind":"para","text":"A lot of preachers and pastors are good at faking their care. While it might fool us, God is not fooled by it. I don’t know how a pastor can fake their care and then tell himself that he is assured by God. Yet I see a lot of pastors tricking themselves into believing this. They don’t have the assurance of God; they have the assurance of self. They even lie to themselves about their lies of care towards you. Honestly, that is the best way to give strong lies. If you can believe your own lie, it wouldn’t be hard for others to believe it too."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"A true child of God will be convicted of their lies. A pastor, for instance, might be good at making you feel as if you really matter to them, but on the inside, he doesn’t really care. If he truly is a child of God, he absolutely will feel convicted for lying. It’s up to him to go to that person and make it right, or to reject the Lord’s prodding and ruin the testimony of faith."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, many pastors will ignore the prodding of God, and it leaves plenty of people with a sour taste in their mouth about the faith. Pastors should not be fake ministers. No man that calls himself a minister should be able to tell people they love them without the burden of proof. If you don’t feel burdened to prove your love and comfort to others, you are likely not genuine in your faith towards God. God proves His love towards His children. He doesn’t just say it."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.","ref":"Romans 5:8"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"The LORD hath appeared of old unto me, saying, Yea, I have loved thee with an everlasting love: therefore with lovingkindness have I drawn thee.","ref":"Jeremiah 31:3"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me: and the life which I now live in the flesh I live by the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and gave himself for me.","ref":"Galatians 2:20"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That Christ may dwell in your hearts by faith; that ye, being rooted and grounded in love, May be able to comprehend with all saints what is the breadth, and length, and depth, and height; And to know the love of Christ, which passeth knowledge, that ye might be filled with all the fulness of God.","ref":"Ephesians 3:17-19"},{"kind":"para","text":"If He proved it, we should too."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we did everything the faith way, we should not have felt condemned about what we say, even if we don’t have action. If we truly mean that we love someone and that we are available for that person, then the Lord will not convict us to prove it. We will WANT to prove it. Real holy love gives us a desire to keep our promises, and real holy desire always finds a way."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should not just be men of THE Word or BY the Word. We should be men of OUR word if we are men of the faith. If people can’t trust our promises, how can they trust our preaching?"}]},{"start":22,"end":22,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If we are genuine in our faith, our promises, and our love towards others, we can come to God in confidence through prayer, knowing that He will be merciful to us because He knows the depths of our hearts. He knows us deeper than our simple words of broken promises. He knows if we were genuine or not. If we were not, we are tricking ourselves into believing that the Lord has a strong relationship with us. If we are genuine, then we know that God has given us the reason and ability to love and serve others rightfully."}]},{"start":23,"end":23,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus actually said this to His disciples:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Master, which is the great commandment in the law? Jesus said unto him, Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind. This is the first and great commandment. And the second is like unto it, Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself. On these two commandments hang all the law and the prophets.","ref":"Matthew 22:36-40"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think there are a lot of people who have had no problem with the “first” commandment (loving God). It’s the second commandment of Christ that people are conflicted by. They will say, “Oh yes, I have loved others. Look at this man in ministry, or that man in leadership. Look how much I have this person who is serving here, and has a faithful testimony.” They might even say, “Look, I love these people. Look how many people I invited to church!” They think that is biblical love. Not according to John."},{"kind":"para","text":"Biblical love is about true connection and unity. It’s about saying what you mean and meaning what you say. That is what real biblical love consists of. It consists of giving life, faith, joy, and purpose to the person you are talking to without any sort of thing in return. Money, ability, and position should not be a prerequisite for friendship in the church. Just being a friendly person and wanting connection should be the only prerequisite for community in the church. If I wanted work friends, I would just get them from my job. That is not the place of the church…"}]},{"start":24,"end":24,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Spirit has a different way of doing life. If men of “faith” do not keep their word, they are not doing life by the Spirit. They are doing life by the world. That is exactly how the world does things, and all it does is hurt people and create division."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should not call ourselves men of faith if we are not willing to keep our word towards others. We should not call ourselves ministers if we only see people as prospects. We are spitting in the face of God by calling ourselves children of God and not loving God’s other children."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21},{"start":22,"end":22},{"start":23,"end":23},{"start":24,"end":24}],"verses":{"1":"Behold, what manner of love the Father hath bestowed upon us, that we should be called the sons of God: therefore the world knoweth us not, because it knew him not.","2":"Beloved, now are we the sons of God, and it doth not yet appear what we shall be: but we know that, when he shall appear, we shall be like him; for we shall see him as he is.","3":"And every man that hath this hope in him purifieth himself, even as he is pure.","4":"Whosoever committeth sin transgresseth also the law: for sin is the transgression of the law.","5":"And ye know that he was manifested to take away our sins; and in him is no sin.","6":"Whosoever abideth in him sinneth not: whosoever sinneth hath not seen him, neither known him.","7":"Little children, let no man deceive you: he that doeth righteousness is righteous, even as he is righteous.","8":"He that committeth sin is of the devil; for the devil sinneth from the beginning. For this purpose the Son of God was manifested, that he might destroy the works of the devil.","9":"Whosoever is born of God doth not commit sin; for his seed remaineth in him: and he cannot sin, because he is born of God.","10":"In this the children of God are manifest, and the children of the devil: whosoever doeth not righteousness is not of God, neither he that loveth not his brother.","11":"For this is the message that ye heard from the beginning, that we should love one another.","12":"Not as Cain, who was of that wicked one, and slew his brother. And wherefore slew he him? Because his own works were evil, and his brother's righteous.","13":"Marvel not, my brethren, if the world hate you.","14":"We know that we have passed from death unto life, because we love the brethren. He that loveth not his brother abideth in death.","15":"Whosoever hateth his brother is a murderer: and ye know that no murderer hath eternal life abiding in him.","16":"Hereby perceive we the love of God, because he laid down his life for us: and we ought to lay down our lives for the brethren.","17":"But whoso hath this world's good, and seeth his brother have need, and shutteth up his bowels of compassion from him, how dwelleth the love of God in him?","18":"My little children, let us not love in word, neither in tongue; but in deed and in truth.","19":"And hereby we know that we are of the truth, and shall assure our hearts before him.","20":"For if our heart condemn us, God is greater than our heart, and knoweth all things.","21":"Beloved, if our heart condemn us not, then have we confidence toward God.","22":"And whatsoever we ask, we receive of him, because we keep his commandments, and do those things that are pleasing in his sight.","23":"And this is his commandment, That we should believe on the name of his Son Jesus Christ, and love one another, as he gave us commandment.","24":"And he that keepeth his commandments dwelleth in him, and he in him. And hereby we know that he abideth in us, by the Spirit which he hath given us."}},
    "4": {"chapter":4,"intro":[],"previously":[", John has been very insistent about proving love in this book of the Bible. In the last chapter, he spent a good portion reminding the church that they are the children of God. He talked about the hope they have in Jesus, what separates them from the rest of the world, how important it is to live with your brothers in Christ in “deed and truth,” and knowing your standing before the Lord. John goes even deeper in this chapter by perfectly illustrating the differences between what real love looks like and what a spirit of error looks like. The world has a love for itself, but people of the faith should have a love that outmatches anything the world can have. There’s a lot of useful information in this chapter regarding loving the people of faith."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":3,"title":"Of God?","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"He is not just calling them “Little children,” but the Spirit of the church should be looked at as such. There are many people who have missed out on their childhood. Sometimes their parents force them to grow up too fast, rather than encourage their youth. Because of this, I think many adults read something like “little children” and think, “That’s a foolish way to act as an adult.” They don’t get it. They missed the awe and wonder of discovering life and newness. They missed what it was like to enjoy little things and take in big moments. They don’t know what it feels like to rest, be comforted, and feel security. These are things that you don’t get when you are an adult."},{"kind":"para","text":"However, when you are born again, you should have a fresh sense of adolescence that comes with it. In the way that you think, in the way that you speak, in the actions you take, and in the decisions you make. Things should be looked at with fresh eyes, as if you never noticed them before. At least for me, the day I came to know the Lord, it was as if the room lit up. I started noticing the things I said. I started noticing the things that others said as well. I started thinking about the things I was doing and wondered if God could ever love someone like me with all my terrible deeds and sin. Then I started studying my Bible as if it were the first time I had ever read a book before. The crazy part was, I started to understand how it affects me. It never did before. Even more so, I started to understand what the preachers were telling me as well! It was like riding my bike for the first time. Everyone told me it was fun, but anytime I got on the bike, I’d just fall over and hurt myself. Once I learned how to ride the bike, however, I understood what they were talking about."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sadly, just like riding a bike, you start to just get used to it at some point. It’s not just about learning to ride; you have to learn to make it more fun. Add some ramps, climb some hills, speed through a trail. I’m talking spiritually here. As children, we trust nearly everything. Unless they are openly and obviously scary, we are willing to usually trust people we are told to trust. As adults, we have learned to test people to see if they can be trusted. In the church, however, we don’t seem to test anything. Except for me. It seems that I am one of very few people who try to test things in a church, and that really upsets the leaders when I do that. I’m not trying to upset anyone; I’m just trying to make sure the environment is safe and the teaching is right for me and my family."},{"kind":"para","text":"John says, “Beloved, you need to test people. Don’t just believe them because they are pastors. Don’t just believe them because they sound fancy. Don’t just believe them because they wear nice clothes and sport fancy watches. Some spirits do not have your best interest in mind.” Honestly, a lot of spirits don’t have your best interest in mind. That should not be said about the church leadership, but I find it often trickles from there…"}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Okay… so I’m probably going to hurt a lot of people here with my thoughts, but this will align best with scripture (as far as I’ve learned, and I still have much to learn in the way of theology and doctrine). John is one hundred percent right here. “Every” means “individually” and “collectively.” It also means “the whole.” So when John says that EVERY spirit who confesses Jesus Christ is come in the flesh is of God, he certainly means every."},{"kind":"para","text":"You say, “Well, the Catholic church believes that, does that mean it’s true?” Yes. “The Mormon church believes that, does that mean they are true?” Yes. “What about the Muslim church?” Yes. However, just because they believe Jesus came in the flesh doesn’t mean they are doctrinally correct. Just because every religion believes in a god doesn’t mean they understand or know the REAL God. But the fact that Jesus Christ came and walked this Earth is not debatable. Even atheists (if they are willing to think for themselves) will admit that there was likely a man named Jesus who walked this Earth. They might not believe Him to be God, but they will likely believe Him to be a good teacher. So if we can start with “Did Jesus come to Earth?” and they agree with it… Well, that knowledge is from God. They have a Godly knowledge, and they probably don’t even know it."},{"kind":"para","text":"But the buck doesn’t stop there. Obviously, in order for them to be saved and changed, they have to believe in the death and resurrection of Jesus too. So the Mormon church (for instance) believes that Jesus came, but they didn’t believe He was God. They believe He BECAME God after He had died and risen. Then they believe you have to work for your salvation afterward. The Catholic church also believes in a works-based faith. They nullify the point of Christ’s ministry and death. But that is not what John wants to focus on. Paul focuses on that very much, but John is keeping it simple. “Let’s start with if they believe that Jesus was a real person and we can go from there. If they believe that, they are of God (to a degree), if they don’t…”"}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"There are plenty of people who deny that Jesus Christ was a real person. They don’t know why they deny it. They don’t even know why they won’t search the evidence. They have just been spoon-fed some sort of information, and instead of looking into the facts themselves, they just trust some guy with a PhD. Actually, sometimes they just trust some guy… that’s as far as they are willing to go. Some guy said something fancy, and now they are tricked and deceived into believing anything else that guy will say."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have recognized that many cults have started this way. I have also recognized that many people are extremely hurt by following false preachers (or antichrists as John calls them here). Eventually, they will fail you. They are humans. Humans fail. Period."}]}]},{"start":4,"end":6,"title":"Truth or Error","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It really baffles me when I see a lot of good, strong men being tricked by evil, weak men. How does a strong, smart young man become so easily led astray? Honestly, I think there are multiple reasons for this."},{"kind":"para","text":"First, they are not planted firmly on the foundation of Jesus Christ as Lord. They might believe that Jesus Christ existed, but they do not treat Him like their Savior. It’s easy for a young man to fall away from the faith when he has not stood firm on the truth. Perhaps he doesn’t fully understand the truth of scripture."},{"kind":"para","text":"I think a lot of people are waiting to hear about the depths of faith, but those things take time. They don’t have the patience, so they look for someone to just teach them SOMETHING."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the time will come when they will not endure sound doctrine; but after their own lusts shall they heap to themselves teachers, having itching ears; And they shall turn away their ears from the truth, and shall be turned unto fables.","ref":"2 Timothy 4:3-4"},{"kind":"para","text":"Paul knew the time “will come,” but it was already there when he was writing the letter. They want to learn something, and they want to be trained in it. So, a lot of people don’t get the speed of faith because they don’t have enough patience to wait for it. Perhaps there is something that someone will throw at me that I have not recognized or don’t know that well. Instead of me saying, “Hmm… That makes a lot of sense, I’m going to throw away everything I have been studying and start following what you say from here on out,” like a lot of people today do, we should say, “Hmm… Well, I don’t know much about this, but I do know that Jesus Christ died for my sins, and He rose again to give me life. I’ll stick with that until the rest is revealed.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"As I’ve said before, core doctrines are non-negotiable. We do NOT waver on core doctrines. If we do, it is because we never had faith to begin with, and they are not “core” to you. Then we have the problem of church leadership. When we are in a position (in the church) to lead people, we don’t continually go out and look for new people. Rather, we disciple the people that are already there. Many churches forsake this ministry. They want more seats filled, rather than more faith-filled. So many young, intelligent, smart men are left without a teacher, and so they go to find someone who will take the time to teach them. I pray to God they find someone who is biblically sound, but many people don’t. They simply find someone who notices them, whether they are sound or not. That is the responsibility of the church leader, and they neglect their God-given responsibility all the time…"},{"kind":"para","text":"I have heard stories of new members coming into the church and faith, and the leaders completely neglect them. They sit at home crying and wondering what happened. They think, “Did they just sell me a bill of goods? Do they actually care about me? I have all this knowledge, and I don’t know what to do with it? Is any of it real? They don’t live like it’s real.” And then they start to reject the church community. I heard about one guy going through this issue, and a church leader said, “Be the change you want to see.” It’s solid advice, but I have a better proposition for you: Next time someone is hurting, and you want to see them come out of it, instead of telling them what they can do to make it better, why don’t YOU (as a leader) SHOW them how to come out of it. You teach by action, not just by words. We spoke about that in the last chapter."},{"kind":"para","text":"Actions matter when it comes to ministers, but ministers (today) hardly EVER do what they say. Don’t get me started on how little they actually care to teach. When you are a child of God, however, you can stand firm on core doctrines and easily overcome the lies of the world. The doctrine of the world might be enticing, but it’s hardly ever rewarding. Usually, if it rewards you, it’s only because it robbed someone else to give you satisfaction. The church should not be in that business…"}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The world loves itself. It is actually pretty good at taking care of its own. If you join the LGBT community, the BLM community, or a political community, you will find a strong bond among its members. However, they are all humans, and they will fail. But the bond certainly is there. People are fully capable of bonding over sinful things, such as drinking and parties, drugs and get-togethers, and even pornography. The point is, there is plenty in this world for people to find connections in sinful things. However, those connections are usually filled with more perverseness, insincere motives, and strongarming. They make a connection with you because they want to get something from you, rather than give something to you. They just want you to agree with them and give their voice more power because there are more people. That is not how a community should thrive."},{"kind":"para","text":"When I became a Christian, I started learning all these things about scripture, religion, and life that I had never seen before. I honestly believed I was the only one who started recognizing these things. I believed that I was the only one who could truly see the truth. Granted, there are some people still who don’t know the simple truths of scripture (and they have been in the faith for quite some time), but I am certainly not the only one who knows these truths. The church community is backed up (or at least should be) by the fact that we all have the same truth given to us, and we all agree to it in one mind."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Fulfil ye my joy, that ye be likeminded, having the same love, being of one accord, of one mind.","ref":"Philippians 2:2"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That ye may with one mind and one mouth glorify God, even the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ.","ref":"Romans 15:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"We all have the mind of Christ. I can go to a completely foreign state, with complete strangers in the church, and still have the same mind as them because we all have experienced the same God. That’s a community. We encourage each other. We help each other when we are down. We wait for those who are hurting. When one falls, we all fall. When one rises, we all rise. It’s in sync. There’s no other community that is like this, especially if it’s over something sinful. There aren’t many church communities that are like this today either, sadly. If the world’s communities and the church’s communities don’t have anything different in them, then they are both of the world."}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It is quite easy to confuse a worldly man. You bring up scripture and the love of the Lord, and it’s like it goes in one ear and comes out the other. I know it did for me. My brain would just shut off when they talked about that stuff. It annoyed me so much. But now that I know the Lord, I understand what they are saying, and they understand what I am saying. The rest of the world doesn’t understand, and they don’t care to understand. That is why a Christian cannot make community with the world. You cannot have truth and lies in the same community. It either has to be all true, or it has to be all lies."},{"kind":"para","text":"If there is a church leader who is lying, that is reason enough to test his spirit. Is it the spirit of truth, or the spirit of error? Test the spirits! Don’t be lazy when it comes to spiritual matters. You can be lazy physically, you can be lazy mentally, but there is never room to be lazy spiritually."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":10,"title":"God's Love","entries":[{"start":7,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have already defined love in this book of the Bible. The thing we must remember is that love is not exclusive to our group of believers. However, I have also noticed that love is not inclusive to our group of believers. We show more love for the lost than we do for our brothers in Christ, and that is not the way in which the church should run. We should love the lost (don’t misconstrue what I’m saying), but we should love the saved more."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus didn’t spend more time with the lost than He did with His disciples. His disciples were always around to help Jesus serve and meet needs. They were learning how to do it by action. Jesus could have simply told them, “This is what you have to do. Now go do it.” But that isn’t what He did. He physically showed them how to do it. If a pastor, preacher, deacon, teacher, or leader of any sort cannot teach you through action, they are liars and will rob you of value. Their end goal (and I know this for a fact because I’ve seen it myself) is to get whatever they can from you, until there’s nothing left for you to give, and then leave you at the wayside defeated and cold."},{"kind":"para","text":"Don’t trust a pastor who only tells you things but doesn’t show you things. Don’t trust any church member (or business, political, or otherwise leader) who will not go alongside you and help you learn. If they only desire to talk and not do, they are looking to steal from you and hurt you. Run. That’s all the warning I can give. Just walk away from those kinds of people. Don’t stick around. You will be a victim, and church abuse is extremely real and terrible."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God showed His love by sending His Son. He showed His love by giving us life through the gift and sacrifice of His only begotten Son. God only has one Son, mind you. Anyone who tells you otherwise is also looking to rob you. They are not of the Spirit of God. They are of the spirit of antichrist."}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Our version of what love is, is a very shallow and depraved version of what real love looks like. Real love is God sending His Son to be a “propitiation” for our sins. “Propitiation” means “appeasing.” So John says that real love is God giving us His Son to literally, physically, and spiritually pay for our sins. He died so that our sin debt can be paid. If this is news to you, you might want to go back and read the gospels. This should be a core doctrine for every person reading this."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus was the payment for sin because He had no sin. He was perfect, and it was only through Him that sins can be forgiven because He bore our sins on the cross. That is love. We can’t do that for someone else. We are not perfect. We cannot bear the sins of another man. We can’t even bear our own sins on our own shoulders. So we can never live up to the same love that God has for us. Neither should we ever want to."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":15,"title":"Loving Others","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Because God has proven His love to us, we should prove our love to the brothers in Christ. John keeps bringing this point up (I believe) because this church was having a problem with loving one another. They probably had a lot of love for the lost, but not a lot of love for the saved. Like I said, there are many churches that struggle with this today."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our love for the saved should be the overflowing trickle of our love for the lost. Arguably, we cannot say we love the lost if we are not showing it to our brothers in Christ first."}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We understand God’s love, yet we have never actually physically seen the Father. However, we can physically see our brothers in Christ, and we have a hard time understanding how to love them. Loving the saints is a much-needed ministry today, and it is the greatest way to show God’s unity and genuine community. If it doesn’t start in the family of God, it will not expand to the lost community."},{"kind":"para","text":"Take the LGBT community for example: they talk about love quite a lot. They have a bad idea of what real love looks like, but they certainly love themselves and their own community. That love cannot trickle out of that community. They horde it and keep it for themselves, and unless you join them, you will not be loved. That is not how a Christian should love."},{"kind":"para","text":"Our love should originate from God, spread to the brothers of Christ, then trickle into the community outside of ours. Our love for one another should be felt by the LGBT community (and any others, I’m just focusing on one community here for simplicity). My point is, love in the Christian household is not exclusive. But it must start in the household of faith first."}]},{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Honestly, if we have a hard time loving our brothers and sisters in the Lord, we probably don’t have the Spirit of God in us. His Spirit produces that kind of love. It produces joy in the work. It produces the light of truth and trust. Church communities should have this without a second thought if they truly have the Spirit of God guiding them."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s funny; I’ve heard some people say, “Sometimes I don’t feel like I’m actually saved. Does that make me a bad Christian?” No. What makes someone a bad Christian is if they let their personal feelings keep them from allowing the Spirit to give love to others. You can be conflicted in your faith and still love others. You don’t simply throw away your faith based on a question. That is so absurd to me."},{"kind":"para","text":"If I give my daughter math homework, and she doesn’t understand it, I’m not going to simply let her throw away the homework and fail a grade. Rather, I hope that she will search for the answers. I give her books, I give her thoughts, I try to teach her, and she is (usually) prepared for the test when it comes. She didn’t just give up because she had a hard question. She confronted it and found a conclusive answer that was satisfying to logic. That is what every person of faith should do too."}]},{"start":14,"end":14,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have not seen it in the flesh, but I have seen it in the scriptures. I also know the Spirit that John speaks about here. I have felt that Spirit. I have followed that Spirit. I have let that Spirit control the outcomes. I have not regretted trusting the Spirit to do what is right. Sometimes it gets me in trouble, but the end results of trusting the Spirit are always rewarding. ALWAYS."},{"kind":"para","text":"But I can testify like John because I have seen the Spirit do miraculous things from small decisions and confusing logic. The Spirit does things that pass our simple understandings. The only thing that makes sense is that scripture is real, true, and evident because the things they said would happen actually ARE happening right before my eyes."},{"kind":"para","text":"This is not to mention I have a changed life because of it, and countless others have proven that as well."}]},{"start":15,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is where the line is drawn for core doctrine. If you do not believe that Jesus is God’s Son, you do not have the Spirit of God in you. That means you are not saved, and you cannot be saved until you believe that. There is only one stipulation to salvation, and that is that Jesus Christ is God in the flesh:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.","ref":"John 1:1"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I and my Father are one.","ref":"John 10:30"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given: and the government shall be upon his shoulder: and his name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, The mighty God, The everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace.","ref":"Isaiah 9:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Thomas answered and said unto him, My Lord and my God.","ref":"John 20:28"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus said unto them, Verily, verily, I say unto you, Before Abraham was, I am.","ref":"John 8:58"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And they said, Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and thou shalt be saved, and thy house.","ref":"Acts 16:31"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Jesus saith unto him, I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me.","ref":"John 14:6"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think we get the point. Salvation comes through Jesus Christ alone. If you try to achieve it any other way, it will be false, and it will not save."}]}]},{"start":16,"end":21,"title":"The Gift of Love","entries":[{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Sacrificial love is a profound concept that requires us to dwell in “love” from the presence of God first and foremost. If we choose to dwell in any other style of love, it will inevitably be selfish. God’s love is the only love that will suffice to live in, as it is eternal and pure. All other forms of love tend to rust over time, losing their essence and depth."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"People judge us all the time. The world loves to judge the Christian and then gets mad when the Christian judges them. It’s actually kind of funny. The only judgment I trust is the judgment of Christians because they have the judgment of God. God will judge the Christians as well. Christians should be very alert to it. But we can have boldness before His judgment if we have the spirit of Christ and the love of God. If we are not dwelling in God’s love and we are not loving the people of the faith, we might want to concern ourselves with what kind of judgment we are going to be facing after this."}]},{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is quite the bold statement. It’s true, but it’s concerning. If I love something, sometimes I am worried or fearful of losing that same thing. I love my wife. I can’t stand the thought of losing her. I love my children. I can’t stand the thought of losing them. It’s not about being fearful of losing them, though. What John speaks of here is a fear of progress. If you love something, you will move forward with it. There are many people who are stalled by fear. They get too scared to go further with what they claim to love, and in the end, they end up losing it anyway."},{"kind":"para","text":"Real love moves forward. Fake love stands still. Real love takes risks. Fake love seeks rewards. Real love sacrifices self. Fake love sacrifices others. Real love motivates others. Fake love keeps them back. Real love is willing to go the distance. Fake love is afraid to move. You see where John is going with this? When we come before the Lord’s judgment, we can come boldly to it if we have had real love for the Lord. Does our love for God take risks for Him, move forward for Him, sacrifice ourselves for Him, motivate others for Him, go the distance for Him? If it didn’t, you should certainly fear coming before Him because you were not perfected in God’s love."},{"kind":"para","text":"This life is the only opportunity you have to perfect your love. That is the only real journey a human being will have to go on. What a dismal thought to think that there are people who never understand, grasp, or search for real love in this world."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"God has made it easy for the Christian to learn love. He showed it first, demonstrated it, and gave us an example. Now, all we have to do is accept it and follow the same guidelines. The “What would Jesus do” crowd is absolutely right. We should look at every and all situations and ask, “What would Jesus do?”"},{"kind":"para","text":"In scripture, we get the full display of Christ’s character. His number one character trait is… (drumroll please)… love! He always put the needs of others first. He tried to make time for his own needs, but that was always interrupted by the needs of others, and instead of complaining about it, he simply went and served. Instead of sending someone else to do it, he went himself. He knew that they needed him, not his disciples. He was pleased to love other people, and we love him because he proved his own love for us first."}]},{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John brought this back up because that is still the main issue of the church. It’s not just about loving God. If we truly love God, that will overflow into the people around us. Our love for God will be the ignition for our love for others."},{"kind":"para","text":"I love my church community. Whether they love me back is up in the air, but I have done what I can as a faithful friend to meet with them, pray for them, and encourage them in the direction that God would have them go. I’ve always been there for them. I’ve always met with them. I’ve always made time for them. I’ve always gone out of my way for them. That is simply because I love them. That is what a person does when they actually love someone. If they don’t… I hate to break it to you, but they don’t love you. You don’t need their love anyway. God gifts people with love, and it is our duty to gift it to others who are saved as well."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":14},{"start":15,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21}],"verses":{"1":"Beloved, believe not every spirit, but try the spirits whether they are of God: because many false prophets are gone out into the world.","2":"Hereby know ye the Spirit of God: Every spirit that confesseth that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh is of God:","3":"And every spirit that confesseth not that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh is not of God: and this is that spirit of antichrist, whereof ye have heard that it should come; and even now already is it in the world.","4":"Ye are of God, little children, and have overcome them: because greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world.","5":"They are of the world: therefore speak they of the world, and the world heareth them.","6":"We are of God: he that knoweth God heareth us; he that is not of God heareth not us. Hereby know we the spirit of truth, and the spirit of error.","7":"Beloved, let us love one another: for love is of God; and every one that loveth is born of God, and knoweth God.","8":"He that loveth not knoweth not God; for God is love.","9":"In this was manifested the love of God toward us, because that God sent his only begotten Son into the world, that we might live through him.","10":"Herein is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son to be the propitiation for our sins.","11":"Beloved, if God so loved us, we ought also to love one another.","12":"No man hath seen God at any time. If we love one another, God dwelleth in us, and his love is perfected in us.","13":"Hereby know we that we dwell in him, and he in us, because he hath given us of his Spirit.","14":"And we have seen and do testify that the Father sent the Son to be the Saviour of the world.","15":"Whosoever shall confess that Jesus is the Son of God, God dwelleth in him, and he in God.","16":"And we have known and believed the love that God hath to us. God is love; and he that dwelleth in love dwelleth in God, and God in him.","17":"Herein is our love made perfect, that we may have boldness in the day of judgment: because as he is, so are we in this world.","18":"There is no fear in love; but perfect love casteth out fear: because fear hath torment. He that feareth is not made perfect in love.","19":"We love him, because he first loved us.","20":"If a man say, I love God, and hateth his brother, he is a liar: for he that loveth not his brother whom he hath seen, how can he love God whom he hath not seen?","21":"And this commandment have we from him, That he who loveth God love his brother also."}},
    "5": {"chapter":5,"intro":[],"previously":["In our last chapter, John spoke strongly about being aware of what is actually from God and what is from the world. He talked about seeking out the spirit of truth and being aware of the spirit of error, which represents the world. He made it very clear that a Christian child should be entrenched in God’s love, so much so that it has no choice but to pour out into his brothers around him, which will then affect the people outside of the church. He reminded the readers that love is a gift that should be stewarded rather than a tool to be hoarded. In this chapter, John will talk about the theme of “life.” He will discuss faith and how it actually holds hands with the Spirit of love, how they should meet together, and what signs of proof the Lord has provided that He loves us and gives us the ability to love others. This last chapter is rich with details of proof and needed guidance for faith and unity."],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":12,"title":"Records of Life","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We have learned some hard-hitting truths as we have studied these chapters of scripture. For me, I often forget about displaying and living in the love of God. I know many pastors and teachers who do the same. They have a one-track mind. They have the obligation (to their churches and their finances) to treat people like they belong, when in all reality, their goal is to just keep you around in case they need you in the future. Some pastors don’t even look at people with THAT much respect. They don’t even want to keep you around if you have been around for a while. They just want new faces."},{"kind":"para","text":"But when we know God’s love, and we know God (which are synonymous with each other), then we have no choice but to love people the way they are supposed to be loved. When we see a man with genuine love for others, we should nurture and nourish that to the fullest extent possible. That is a rare treat to a church. But that is the way in which every child of God should behave towards their brothers in the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"We have been trading classes in the church house today. Instead of “how can I give more,” it’s “how can I get more?” Instead of “how can I serve more,” it’s “how can I be served more?” Instead of “how can I encourage more,” it’s “how can I get rid of them?” Instead of “how can I change a life,” it’s “I hope God will do something about that person.” Instead of “how can I keep people from leaving,” it’s “If they want to leave, I’ll help them find a different church.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"Real leaders don’t see people as tools to be used for the ministry. Real leaders see future potential for a man, sure, but they see others as valuable and unique souls with intention and purpose in both life and ministry."}]},{"start":2,"end":2,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"We love God, therefore we should love God’s people. We might not always agree on everything, nor might we always see eye-to-eye. We might need some work and time to be built up and helped along. But in the end, we should still have a deep love for one another. We have already talked about what real love looks like, but in case you need a refresher, it’s not about what you see or a person’s work ethic. No, we love someone because God loves us (first). We show that love by sacrificing (whether time, money, or self-ambitions is between you and the Lord). We show love by giving (time, money, or self-ambitions). We should love by going (alongside the person in question, and we encourage and help them reach their potential for the Lord). We should love people by being a compass and voice of reason."},{"kind":"para","text":"I have met pastors who say they love me, and their version of that is to tell me what they think of me, and then pull me out of every sort of God-given ministry alive. Their version of love is to tell me how valuable (or invaluable) I am to ministry. I don’t want your love to be only focused on my ministry ethics… That’s not real love. Real love is “longsuffering” and “gentle.” Real love is what the fruit of God’s spirit produces. That is why it starts in “love.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith, Meekness, temperance: against such there is no law.","ref":"Galatians 5:22-23"},{"kind":"para","text":"Real, genuine Godly love will produce joy, which will give a person peace and allow them to be longsuffering, gentle, good, and filled with faith, among other qualities. The point is, if we want to be genuine in our love, we must detach ourselves from the ministry outlook and meet with people down in the mud for a season. Real love is willing to put goals to the side so people can catch up."}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might say, “Well Jonathan, if real love waits for goals, and it’s not a work that must be done, then what would you call ‘keeping his commandments’?” Good question. We have two different laws of scripture. We have the law of God (the ten commandments), and then we have the law of love (the Christ commandments). Christ only gave two commands to His followers. It was to love God and love others. That is it. It’s not grievous. We have already talked about it, so I won’t spend much time on the point. But the fact is, a Christian’s life should be exemplified in those very simple two commandments. Love God (that is to spend as much time with God as possible and sit with Him), and love others (that is to spend as much time with others as possible and sit with them). Too many people can do one without the other, and love falls to the wayside."}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The Beatles famously said, “All you need is love!” However, John suggests, “Well… actually, you need faith.” Love is certainly involved in overcoming the world, but faith is the foundation of love. You might argue, “Hey! Wait a second, you said love was the driving factor!” Yes, love does drive faith. However, love also cannot exist without faith. I can love my wife without faith, but I cannot have faith in my wife without love. Similarly, I can love the Lord without faith, but I cannot have faith in the Lord without love. I can say, “God, I love you, but I don’t know if you’re doing the right thing here.” However, I cannot say, “God, I have faith in you, but I don’t love you for this.” That doesn’t make sense."},{"kind":"para","text":"Let me put it this way: when we hear the gospel for the first time, it initiates “faith” first, not love. When we have faith, we suddenly understand the love of God, which drives us into a deeper faith afterward. Faith is the opening to overcoming the world, and love is the victory that proves faith’s power."}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Some people believe that their personal power and finances are the key to overcoming the world. However, they have not overcome the world through those things. Rather, that only proves that they have joined the world. To overcome the world, you will need something far more powerful than what you find in the world."},{"kind":"para","text":"“I have food, water, shelter, and clothes. That’s a pretty good sign of overcoming the world to me,” you might say. But you eat God’s food, drink God’s water, live in houses made out of God’s elements, and wear clothes made by God’s people. You have not done anything but become part of the world. No, you need something out of this world to overcome the world. That power only belongs to the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s not about being the most wealthy man in the world. It’s not even about being the only man in the world. If you were the last one alive, you still would not have overcome the world. You need Jesus. He is the only man who has ever overcome the world. He is the Son of God and the Savior of mankind. He came back to life, proving that there is no power in this world that is more capable than Him."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Looking unto Jesus the author and finisher of our faith; who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising the shame, and is set down at the right hand of the throne of God.","ref":"Hebrews 12:2"}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John was present the day that Jesus gave His life. He provides a detailed explanation of what happened:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But when they came to Jesus, and saw that he was dead already, they brake not his legs: But one of the soldiers with a spear pierced his side, and forthwith came there out blood and water. And he that saw it bare record, and his record is true: and he knoweth that he saith true, that ye might believe.","ref":"John 19:33-35"},{"kind":"para","text":"In scripture, water has always been a symbol of the Word of God. God’s Word refreshes and nourishes people. Blood has always spoken about death. The same manner can be thought of here. The Spirit of God reveals the truth to us."},{"kind":"para","text":"I had heard about the crucifixion of Jesus for most of my life. I never quite understood it, or really even cared. The Spirit did not reveal anything to me. I didn’t have the Spirit because I didn’t personally know Christ. It wasn’t until the day that I simply trusted in Christ that the Spirit finally revealed to me what I knew all along. I finally understood the depths of God’s sacrifice and love. His Spirit granted me the ability to not just hear about the death of my Savior, but to actually witness it with my own eyes. Obviously not physically, but through His Word. The depths of that sacrifice are far more astounding when the Spirit reveals the truth to you."}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"I have spent a great deal of effort writing about why this verse is missing in most modern texts and translations of scripture. I won’t spend too much time looking at that, but I will spend a great deal of time examining the representation (and promises of wisdom) here. I will say that many scholars can be right, and this verse didn’t appear in the earlier texts. Why that matters, I don’t know. It’s written in the majority text, just not the earliest texts. Whatever that might mean to you."},{"kind":"para","text":"The scripture itself reveals an enormous amount of truth about the Godhead that can’t be overlooked, and it is one hundred percent accurate to the rest of scripture (and scripture as a whole). This single verse reveals the truth of God’s Word from beginning to end. If John didn’t originally write this (which we may never know), then whoever did was certainly inspired by God’s Spirit to write it in perfection."},{"kind":"para","text":"There are three that bear record in “heaven.” These things we will not see for sure until we get into the afterlife. The three are “the Father” God. Throughout scripture, we get a representation of God in many different fashions. But one that continually comes up for the reader is His acclamation to being our “Father.” Not just our judge. Not just our creator. Our Father."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But to us there is but one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we in him; and one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him.","ref":"1 Corinthians 8:6"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But now, O LORD, thou art our father; we are the clay, and thou our potter; and we all are the work of thy hand.","ref":"Isaiah 64:8"},{"kind":"para","text":"The second Heavenly record is \"The Word.\" Again, this attributes to the Son."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us, (and we beheld his glory, the glory as of the only begotten of the Father,) full of grace and truth.","ref":"John 1:14"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"I and my Father are one.","ref":"John 10:30"},{"kind":"para","text":"We have studied this quite a bit in this letter and throughout this commentary. I don’t think we have to question the legitimacy of Jesus Christ being God in the flesh at this point. If we still question it, it’s simply because we are willfully ignorant of every sort of truth and proof out there. I’ve given it all with as much effort as I possibly can. The third record is the “Holy Ghost.”"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"But the Comforter, which is the Holy Ghost, whom the Father will send in my name, he shall teach you all things, and bring all things to your remembrance, whatsoever I have said unto you.","ref":"John 14:26"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy he saved us, by the washing of regeneration, and renewing of the Holy Ghost;","ref":"Titus 3:5"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Likewise the Spirit also helpeth our infirmities: for we know not what we should pray for as we ought: but the Spirit itself maketh intercession for us with groanings which cannot be uttered. And he that searcheth the hearts knoweth what is the mind of the Spirit, because he maketh intercession for the saints according to the will of God.","ref":"Romans 8:26-27"},{"kind":"para","text":"The Spirit of God has revealed itself several times to us throughout scripture. Now, John says that there are three that bear record “in heaven,” and according to scholars, the three that bear record were not given by John. It is simply stated, “There are three that bear record in Heaven,” and then John supposedly moves on. If I were a scribe, I would wonder why John didn’t finish his thought. Perhaps John wrote down the three, or perhaps it was a scholar. Either way, it’s alluding to eternal proof of God’s love and salvation for mankind."},{"kind":"para","text":"What I question is not the legitimacy of this verse. What I question is why John calls them a “heavenly” record. There’s not just a “Heavenly” record of this. This is proven throughout history and throughout scripture. The record is literally in our hands. But I suppose the record of Heaven is only given by faith because we cannot physically prove Jesus Christ is living. We cannot physically prove that God is in control. We cannot physically prove that the Holy Spirit lives in a man. However, John gives us things we CAN prove."}]},{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You can witness the Spirit of a man. You can see the countenance of someone who is experiencing something. You can look at someone and say, “That man is alive. He’s not dead.” How? Well, he is still breathing. There’s a spirit in him. If there was not, you would probably think that man was dead or at least mentally gone. A man without a Spirit will not give much effort to do anything. He probably wouldn’t give much effort to even leave the home. Every person has a Spirit. People without the Lord have dead spirits. They are zombies. But when you have God in your life, the Spirit is revived, and you become a living being. Genuine and real."},{"kind":"para","text":"The second “witness” to the legitimacy of a man’s faith is “the water.” This can be taken two-fold. It could talk about baptism or it could talk about God’s Word. Again, water has always represented God’s Word. Even when we get baptized today, it’s still just a representation of us agreeing with God’s Word. There is no special power coming out of a baptism. The special power comes out of faith in God’s Word alone."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"So then faith cometh by hearing, and hearing by the word of God.","ref":"Romans 10:17"},{"kind":"para","text":"God’s Word provides proof of God’s presence in this world. If we are not willing to search it ourselves, we should not think that we will somehow run into the truth by accident. Ignorant people will wait for the truth to come to them, while wise people will seek out the truth until there is a final answer. The third witness on Earth is “the blood.” We have already discussed how the blood is proof of death. It’s also proof of life! Blood is supposed to stay in your body, not come out of it. If it’s in your body, you are alive. Congratulations! If it pours out of your body, that’s not a good sign. The Lord’s body lost blood, and then His blood circulated again three days later. That’s according to the witnesses here on Earth, the witnesses writing this testimony, and the witnesses who have been changed and the truth has been revealed to today. All three of these things are proven testimonies. It is not up to us to prove them anymore, because we have given every sort of proof possible. It is up to us to believe them. Faith is accepting the record—all six records. If you don’t believe the records here on Earth, you will certainly find the Heavenly records to be true. But by that point, it will be too late, and you will leave nothing behind on this Earth except stale, bitter tastes in people’s memories."}]},{"start":9,"end":9,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This verse (9) is a great example of why verse 7 belongs so well here. We can take the witnesses of verse 8 (which are arguably the witnesses of men), but the witnesses of verse 7 are far more secure and profitable. We can observe spirits, water, and blood with our own eyes. Sure. But we can observe the Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost with our own lives, and that will be far more provable than what we simply see. People say, “Seeing is believing.” That is so small-minded to me. Seeing (alone) is not enough for me. Doing is far more assertive than just saying I saw something."},{"kind":"para","text":"If someone came to me and said, “I just learned how to fly! Do you want to know how too?” and I said, “Yes! Show me how!” and he pulled out a book and told me to read it to learn… I’m not going to believe that guy. I want that guy to fly right in front of me if I’m going to believe it. I want that guy to literally fly with me if it’s true. I treat God’s Word the same. I may not be able to go back in time and see Christ’s death, but the Spirit is a witness, right? If the Spirit is a witness, I want to observe that not just with my eyes. I want to observe the Spirit’s witness through His leading and action. I want to be a part of it if it’s true. To me, that’s the best way to believe something. Testing something to see if it’s true, rather than just seeing someone else do it."},{"kind":"para","text":"I used to love magic when I was younger. I still enjoy it today. I liked the sleight of hand stuff. I did a lot of card tricks and picked a lot of pockets. It was fun. I never stole anything, so relax. I always gave them back their things (whether keys, or wallet, or watch, whatever I could get my hands on). I could have been sufficient to just watch it on TV and be amazed every time they did it, but that was not enough for me. I needed to learn how to do it too. That required me to figure out how it was done, practice how they did it, then go into the world and try it out myself. If I do magic today, it will usually only be for a humorous purpose. But the point is, I spend time learning it so I can do it, and not just see it."},{"kind":"para","text":"When we become Christians, it should be the same thing for us. Many people will reject Christianity because there are not a lot of people who are willing to do what Christ says, instead of just telling people to read their Bibles for themselves. I want to be an observable example of Christianity, not just a preacher behind the pulpit. You can’t share genuine faith and love when you are only seen on stage and in the back shaking hands on Sundays…"}]},{"start":10,"end":10,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"John is simplifying the gospel here and will simplify it even more in a moment. The record is given, and the witnesses are established. There are six proofs against a man in this world, not even including the proof of the church. It’s just the proof of historical fact and undeniable eyewitness evidence, followed by the observable tangible lifestyle proof of a believer. If you cannot accept all of that as proof, you are certainly a lost cause (intentionally at that). Your pride will be the end of you, and you will never get to experience the joy and love that John is writing about here. Faith is the key. Love is the ignition. Joy is the engine."}]},{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The final record is this: God has given us, promised us, and proven to us that eternal life is a gift given by His Son. Believe the record or don’t believe the record. In the end, the truth remains the truth whether people want to follow or believe it or not. John will really simplify the gospel now:"}]},{"start":12,"end":12,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"No matter what language you read this verse in, it is as simple as that. You either have the Son, or you don’t have the Son. If you have the Son of God, you have life. If you don’t have the Son of God, you don’t have life. I don’t know how to simplify it any better than that."}]}]},{"start":13,"end":17,"title":"Sin to Death","entries":[{"start":13,"end":13,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This has to be one of the most peaceful verses written in scripture. Of course, Jesus has given us a lot of things to bring us peace. His words are peace. They are coated in peace. But John gives us all an extreme sense of assurance here. Every believer has a promise here."},{"kind":"para","text":"If you believe in the name (just the name) of the Son of God (that name is Jesus), you can know (that is one hundred percent positivity) that you have eternal life. It’s only by believing in the name of the Son of God. His name is not Mohammad. It’s not Joseph Smith. It’s not Buddha or Siddhartha Gautama. It’s not the Apostle Peter. It’s not the Virgin Mary. It is absolutely, and undoubtedly, the name Jesus."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"That at the name of Jesus every knee should bow, of things in heaven, and things in earth, and things under the earth; And that every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father.","ref":"Philippians 2:10-11"},{"kind":"para","text":"If you want absolute assurance of salvation, there is no other name to put your trust in."}]},{"start":14,"end":15,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"The key to this verse is asking “in His name.” We end our prayers with “In Jesus’ name,” but our prayers are not always prayed “In Jesus’ will.” Because of that, we sometimes pray for things that are not pleasing to the Lord, and so we don’t get what we are asking for. It must align with glorifying God and pleasing the Father if we are going to ask. I have prayed for many people’s health problems. Some of them were pretty sure to fail. Recently, I lost a very good faithful friend who constantly prayed for me. I prayed so deeply for his health to get better. I prayed that God would do a miracle. God did not. That was not in His will this time. I know my friend is with the Lord rejoicing, and I know he would much rather be there, but it still hurts to see God answer prayers in the opposite way you desire. But it’s not up to me to change the will of God in my prayer."},{"kind":"para","text":"Prayer should only do one thing. It’s not to wish for something better. Prayer should not change the will of God. Prayer should change the will of the person who is using it. If we are praying to God about something, God will want us to pray according to His will, not ours. When I prayed for my friend, I prayed that the Lord would do what was right. I said, “Father, I want my friend to receive a miraculous healing. I want him to get better. I don’t want him to leave this Earth just yet. I know he is in pain. I know he can’t breathe. I pray that you would give him comfort, help the doctors to heal him, Lord. Give him and his family a miracle…” I could have ended my prayers like this, and when the Lord didn’t answer them the way I wanted, be very upset with Him. Instead, I ended them with “…however, God, you know what is right. I’m trusting that you will do what is right. I’m trusting your choice in His life’s matter. However you take that pain away, Lord, is absolutely up to you. Just give him rest.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"I trust God’s answers to prayer, even if He doesn’t answer them the way I hoped He would. He always knows what is best, and we can always depend on Him to do what is absolutely perfect and right."}]},{"start":16,"end":16,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This might seem like a bit of a whirlwind if you read this verse out of context. Obviously, we have to look at what we have already read prior to this to understand what John is getting at here."},{"kind":"para","text":"“Brother” insinuates that this man is a saved man. “Death” insinuates physical dying. A saved man does not spiritually die; he can only physically die. There are a lot of sins that can be committed by saved men that lead them to death. Aaron’s sons were examples of this when they made a mistake in the first service to the Lord."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And Nadab and Abihu, the sons of Aaron, took either of them his censer, and put fire therein, and put incense thereon, and offered strange fire before the LORD, which he commanded them not. And there went out fire from the LORD, and devoured them, and they died before the LORD.","ref":"Leviticus 10:1-2"},{"kind":"para","text":"Even Moses and Aaron are examples of this. They both physically died outside the promised land because of their disobedience to the Lord. Ananias and Sapphira also died physically. Spiritually, it is up for debate, but for sure physically, because of their lie in the book of Acts (chapter 5 if you are so interested). Perhaps the sin unto death for others is very different. For my friend, his sin unto death could have been the many years of smoking and drinking he endured. It was a very difficult and tricky thing for him to overcome, even after his salvation. That might have been what led to his death."},{"kind":"para","text":"Sometimes there is a sin in an individual’s life that simply leads them to death, whether saved or not saved. In this instance, there are people dying from sins of sorts in the church (probably from the sin of disunity or seeds of discord) that God will deal with in His own way. Whether by death or by setting them aside, God will somehow deal with them. The “sin unto death” is likely just a reference to continually disgracing God on Earth through actions, motives, or pain towards others. The church that John is writing to had a hard time loving one another. Perhaps that is reason enough for John to make this point here."}]},{"start":17,"end":17,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"You might say, “What sin is NOT unto death?” Well, you are a sinner, aren’t you? I am a sinner. Yet we are still both alive. So we have not physically died from our sins (yet). Though the wages of ALL sin is death:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.","ref":"Romans 6:23"},{"kind":"para","text":"The gift of God is eternal life to the saved sinner."}]}]},{"start":18,"end":21,"title":"Satan's Whisper","entries":[{"start":18,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Satan is always around a child of God. Honestly, I think Satan has attacked me more since becoming a Christian than before I was one. It really irritates him that I have given my life to Christ. Even in these notes (while I’m writing about the love of the brothers), Satan’s voice is telling me how much of a waste all of this is. “No one will read this,” he says. “No one will care.” “No one will change.” “The church is dead.” “The saints are gone.” “Give up now.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"He, however, can’t touch me. He can whisper plenty of things into my ears, but he can’t touch my faith. It’s not for him to dictate. It’s not even for ME to dictate. I gave my faith to God. God controls it now. Even if God does things that really hurt me, I cannot just remove my faith away from Him. I already know He’s real, and I already know the truth. It would be impossible for me to deny it now."},{"kind":"para","text":"Christians might sin, but they cannot do it willfully. They know what is wrong, and they will have to sin through conviction. But they can’t leave their faith behind when they do it. They will always know the truth, no matter how hurt their faith might give them."}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"If you don’t feel like an outcast in your faith, it’s likely that you don’t have a genuine faith. That’s not me accusing you of anything; it’s just the blatant truth according to God’s Word. You should not fit into this world anywhere. That doesn’t mean you cannot spend time with people outside of the faith; it just means you will know you don’t belong with them, and you will always feel like an outcast."},{"kind":"para","text":"You don’t think like them anymore. You don’t speak like them anymore. You don’t do what they do anymore. You have a Spirit always reminding you of who you belong to. That’s not a bad thing; it just makes interactions with sinful things much more uncomfortable. It’s not even because you live by a set of rules. It’s because the Lord convicts you of your sin, and you don’t want to intentionally and purposely hurt the Lord."},{"kind":"para","text":"How can you intentionally hurt God after He has proven His love to you? If you can do that without remorse for His love, then you never really knew His love to begin with."}]},{"start":20,"end":20,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Another key record that John gives here is that we can know God. It’s not just about knowing we are saved by the promises and records of God. It’s about knowing the character of God, growing in the character of God, spending time with the person of God, and actually conversing with the Lord. We are able to be led by the calling and power of God. We can have a deep relationship with the Lord while we are here on Earth, and it will be an even deeper relationship when we get to see Him face to face. For now, the truth is (if we are in Him) we can get to know Him, and we get to love others because of it."}]},{"start":21,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This might seem like a weird thing to end this letter off with, but I think it fits perfectly. Idols (I truly believe) are the number one sin that every human being struggles with. People make money their idol, family their idol, themselves their idol, power their idol, position, fame, and material possession. There are a lot of idols in this world. It just takes a small amount of allowance for it to become a mighty big problem. Perhaps that is what their main issue was. They couldn’t love the saints correctly because they were loving something else more. It was not the Lord they were loving more, that’s for sure. They were likely loving the lost more than they even loved the Lord. Maybe they loved their work more than the Lord? They likely loved their traditions and religious activities far more than they loved the Lord. That seems to be the major theme of most religious groups today. They love doing religious stuff more than getting to know God deeper. They would rather work than sit. They would rather repeat simple phrases than have a heart-to-heart with God. Religion and service can become an idol if people are not careful. If these things become an idol, all you will be left with after serving these things is a lack of love for the saints. John was clever about adding this here at the end. We all need to keep ourselves from idols and be very cautious about what idols are in our lives right now."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":2,"end":2},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":9,"end":9},{"start":10,"end":10},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":12,"end":12},{"start":13,"end":13},{"start":14,"end":15},{"start":16,"end":16},{"start":17,"end":17},{"start":18,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":20},{"start":21,"end":21}],"verses":{"1":"Whosoever believeth that Jesus is the Christ is born of God: and every one that loveth him that begat loveth him also that is begotten of him.","2":"By this we know that we love the children of God, when we love God, and keep his commandments.","3":"For this is the love of God, that we keep his commandments: and his commandments are not grievous.","4":"For whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world: and this is the victory that overcometh the world, even our faith.","5":"Who is he that overcometh the world, but he that believeth that Jesus is the Son of God?","6":"This is he that came by water and blood, even Jesus Christ; not by water only, but by water and blood. And it is the Spirit that beareth witness, because the Spirit is truth.","7":"For there are three that bear record in heaven, the Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost: and these three are one.","8":"And there are three that bear witness in earth, the Spirit, and the water, and the blood: and these three agree in one.","9":"If we receive the witness of men, the witness of God is greater: for this is the witness of God which he hath testified of his Son.","10":"He that believeth on the Son of God hath the witness in himself: he that believeth not God hath made him a liar; because he believeth not the record that God gave of his Son.","11":"And this is the record, that God hath given to us eternal life, and this life is in his Son.","12":"He that hath the Son hath life; and he that hath not the Son of God hath not life.","13":"These things have I written unto you that believe on the name of the Son of God; that ye may know that ye have eternal life, and that ye may believe on the name of the Son of God.","14":"And this is the confidence that we have in him, that, if we ask any thing according to his will, he heareth us:","15":"And if we know that he hear us, whatsoever we ask, we know that we have the petitions that we desired of him.","16":"If any man see his brother sin a sin which is not unto death, he shall ask, and he shall give him life for them that sin not unto death. There is a sin unto death: I do not say that he shall pray for it.","17":"All unrighteousness is sin: and there is a sin not unto death.","18":"We know that whosoever is born of God sinneth not; but he that is begotten of God keepeth himself, and that wicked one toucheth him not.","19":"And we know that we are of God, and the whole world lieth in wickedness.","20":"And we know that the Son of God is come, and hath given us an understanding, that we may know him that is true, and we are in him that is true, even in his Son Jesus Christ. This is the true God, and eternal life.","21":"Little children, keep yourselves from idols. Amen."}},
  },
};
